1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,970
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:03,930
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,930 --> 00:00:07,360
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:15,040
SARA BEERY: So, today, we're going

5
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:18,520
to start off the first of a series of lectures

6
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:21,400
on machine learning architectures.

7
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:27,760
And so moving beyond the very vanilla architecture

8
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:29,360
we've been talking about so far, which

9
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:31,480
is the multilayer perceptron, and starting

10
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:33,840
to discuss different ways that we

11
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:37,160
can encode structure or essentially build hypotheses

12
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:41,000
about the space of models that we want to optimize over

13
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,600
directly into the architecture of the model

14
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:46,320
that we're going to optimize.

15
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:48,540
So what that's going to look like today-- first,

16
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:51,760
we're going to have a broader discussion about why

17
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:53,560
we want to build better architectures

18
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:56,400
and what makes an architecture better.

19
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:59,000
And then we're going to do a bit of a deep dive

20
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,420
on convolutional layers as one specific mechanism you can build

21
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:05,820
into an architecture, one that works particularly

22
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:07,860
well for grids.

23
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:09,680
And this might be a review for some of you,

24
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:12,705
but we're still going to spend some time on it.

25
00:01:12,705 --> 00:01:14,580
Then we're going to talk a bit about pyramids

26
00:01:14,580 --> 00:01:16,900
and the idea of receptive field.

27
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,700
Then I'm going to go through a bit of a high-level overview

28
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:22,460
of a bunch of different architectures

29
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:24,580
and architecture components that have

30
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:28,780
made up a lot of the progress in the last 10 years or so.

31
00:01:28,780 --> 00:01:32,140
And then I'm going to finish with a first touch

32
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:34,240
on neural fields and positional encoding.

33
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:36,380
So we'll talk a lot more about this

34
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:39,980
when we get into lectures on transformers later on.

35
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:44,100
So this is what we've been talking about so far,

36
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:46,500
the multilayer perceptron.

37
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:50,340
So this is, at a high level, the building block

38
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:52,740
is this linear combination of neurons

39
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:55,220
or linear combination of inputs, a neuron

40
00:01:55,220 --> 00:01:57,540
wise or a point-wise non-linearity and then

41
00:01:57,540 --> 00:01:59,640
another linear combination.

42
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:02,320
And one of the benefits of this, like got described

43
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:04,640
in the last lecture, is the fact that it can--

44
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:06,180
it's a universal approximator.

45
00:02:06,180 --> 00:02:08,039
You can build any model and prove

46
00:02:08,039 --> 00:02:12,560
that you can build any model with this type of architecture.

47
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:17,640
Another benefit of a multi-layer perceptron is it's quite simple.

48
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:19,320
And one of the things this simplicity

49
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:22,240
gives you is the ability to write really elegant theory

50
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:22,980
about this model.

51
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:24,160
And this is one of the only models

52
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:26,080
that we actually have really elegant theory

53
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:28,873
for because the complexity is such

54
00:02:28,873 --> 00:02:30,540
that we can make reasonable assumptions,

55
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:35,240
it maps well into our mathematical tool blocks.

56
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:38,360
And then another huge benefit of the multi-layer perceptron

57
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:42,360
is it's what we call embarrassingly parallel.

58
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:45,600
Essentially, this means that this type of architecture

59
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:47,440
can be really easily parallelized

60
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:49,320
in terms of computation, which means

61
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:51,520
we can make use of things like GPUs

62
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:54,660
that enable a lot of parallel multiplications.

63
00:02:54,660 --> 00:02:57,100
So one obvious example of that is

64
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:00,460
that that neuron-wise or that point-wise non-linearity, that's

65
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:03,340
calculated independently for every single output

66
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:04,520
neuron of that first layer.

67
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:06,437
And so that can happen completely in parallel.

68
00:03:06,437 --> 00:03:09,300
There's no dependence across the data points.

69
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:13,300
Another nice example is that linear combination of neurons,

70
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:16,740
that densely connected layer or that weight,

71
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:19,900
that can happen in parallel across a very large,

72
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:21,740
in some cases, batch of data.

73
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:25,140
That's done independently per data point.

74
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:26,580
So what are some of the bad things

75
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:28,900
about the multilayer perceptron?

76
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:34,160
So first one is that it has very weak inductive biases.

77
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:38,640
And so what we mean by this is that the model itself,

78
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:40,120
it's a universal approximator.

79
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:43,420
But there's not a lot of structure or intuition baked

80
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:45,140
into this model.

81
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:48,820
It doesn't assume much about the structure of the model

82
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:51,860
you want to learn coming out.

83
00:03:51,860 --> 00:03:53,820
And so one of the things that implies

84
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:57,520
is that though you can use this model--

85
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:01,000
so you can use a multilayer perceptron to approximate any

86
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,720
of mathematical function.

87
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:09,120
It might be very difficult to do so given the data that you have.

88
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:12,080
It's very sample inefficient or data hungry.

89
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:14,360
This basically means that in order

90
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:16,880
to get this multilayer perceptron to learn maybe

91
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:21,279
a very complex function, you might require so much data

92
00:04:21,279 --> 00:04:22,960
that it's not actually tractable,

93
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:25,000
like more data than exists on Earth to be

94
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,160
able to really generalize and robustly

95
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:31,480
learn a function with this particular architecture that

96
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:33,440
generalizes well.

97
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:36,520
And then another downside is that dense layers, those fully

98
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:39,800
connected layers, particularly if you want a model that's

99
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:42,480
very wide because maybe the function you need to approximate

100
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:45,120
is quite complex, those can be really expensive.

101
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:48,960
If your input data is an image, for example, a high resolution

102
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:52,280
image that you might take on your iPhone today,

103
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:55,510
then you need to flatten that out into one long vector.

104
00:04:55,510 --> 00:04:59,090
That's going to be thousands of input dimensions and 1,000

105
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:02,450
by 1,000 multiplication in a dense way is a very expensive

106
00:05:02,450 --> 00:05:04,690
computational cost.

107
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:08,672
So why use other architectures?

108
00:05:08,672 --> 00:05:11,130
Well, one, we might want to address some of those downsides

109
00:05:11,130 --> 00:05:12,370
that we talked about.

110
00:05:12,370 --> 00:05:18,330
But let's take a meta view of this and zoom out a little.

111
00:05:18,330 --> 00:05:21,730
So if this is the set of all possible mappings from X

112
00:05:21,730 --> 00:05:23,290
to Y-- so this is like all functions

113
00:05:23,290 --> 00:05:26,690
is contained in this little gray box.

114
00:05:26,690 --> 00:05:29,410
And maybe this is the true solution we're trying to learn.

115
00:05:29,410 --> 00:05:36,010
This is the correct model for our problem.

116
00:05:36,010 --> 00:05:40,290
Then possibly given any data set that we're

117
00:05:40,290 --> 00:05:42,490
going to learn from, that data set

118
00:05:42,490 --> 00:05:44,410
will constrain the space a bit.

119
00:05:44,410 --> 00:05:47,810
So not every model will fit the data

120
00:05:47,810 --> 00:05:50,170
that we're using to learn the data.

121
00:05:50,170 --> 00:05:53,190
And so now maybe you have this subset

122
00:05:53,190 --> 00:05:56,390
of all mappings that actually fits your training data.

123
00:05:56,390 --> 00:05:58,270
And then within that, based on something

124
00:05:58,270 --> 00:06:01,310
about your optimization scheme, you learn some solution,

125
00:06:01,310 --> 00:06:03,113
and it will be in that space.

126
00:06:03,113 --> 00:06:05,030
It will fit the data, but it won't necessarily

127
00:06:05,030 --> 00:06:08,510
be very close to the true solution.

128
00:06:08,510 --> 00:06:12,690
So what would happen to this high-level diagram,

129
00:06:12,690 --> 00:06:15,630
if we add more data?

130
00:06:15,630 --> 00:06:16,230
Anyone?

131
00:06:16,230 --> 00:06:18,580
What would happen?

132
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:20,855
Mm-hmm?

133
00:06:20,855 --> 00:06:22,310
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

134
00:06:23,447 --> 00:06:24,530
SARA BEERY: Yeah, exactly.

135
00:06:24,530 --> 00:06:28,530
So that green space, that'll get smaller.

136
00:06:28,530 --> 00:06:30,790
There will be fewer models that fit all of the data

137
00:06:30,790 --> 00:06:31,930
if we have more data.

138
00:06:31,930 --> 00:06:33,770
So that's kind of nice.

139
00:06:33,770 --> 00:06:36,403
So if you can add more data, then potentially you

140
00:06:36,403 --> 00:06:38,070
shrink the space you're optimizing over,

141
00:06:38,070 --> 00:06:40,670
and then you'll learn a maybe more optimal solution

142
00:06:40,670 --> 00:06:42,790
or something that's closer to the true solution

143
00:06:42,790 --> 00:06:44,750
because the space is different.

144
00:06:44,750 --> 00:06:48,230
But what if you don't have more data?

145
00:06:48,230 --> 00:06:49,870
That's not an option for you.

146
00:06:49,870 --> 00:06:55,210
So then another alternative is to define a hypothesis space.

147
00:06:55,210 --> 00:06:58,170
And this is adding some structure into the model itself.

148
00:06:58,170 --> 00:07:00,130
And that hypothesis space is essentially

149
00:07:00,130 --> 00:07:03,810
going to shine a spotlight on the space of all possible models

150
00:07:03,810 --> 00:07:06,150
is the way we've kind of denoted it here.

151
00:07:06,150 --> 00:07:10,490
But essentially, it's a stronger inductive bias or a stronger

152
00:07:10,490 --> 00:07:14,370
prior over what we think the model is

153
00:07:14,370 --> 00:07:16,130
going to take the form of.

154
00:07:16,130 --> 00:07:19,010
And then if you have that intersection of the models that

155
00:07:19,010 --> 00:07:21,270
fit the data and your inductive hypothesis,

156
00:07:21,270 --> 00:07:26,070
then you might actually be able to use less data,

157
00:07:26,070 --> 00:07:28,690
but still find a better architecture.

158
00:07:28,690 --> 00:07:31,210
So the idea is if we can pin down

159
00:07:31,210 --> 00:07:33,610
or if we can get closer to the true model

160
00:07:33,610 --> 00:07:36,255
by either adding more data or by using

161
00:07:36,255 --> 00:07:37,630
a more constrained architecture--

162
00:07:37,630 --> 00:07:39,755
and you can imagine that the combination of the two

163
00:07:39,755 --> 00:07:40,810
would be even better.

164
00:07:40,810 --> 00:07:42,950
So more data helps.

165
00:07:42,950 --> 00:07:45,850
Better architecture or stronger inductive bias helps.

166
00:07:45,850 --> 00:07:50,870
And those two things combined is ideal.

167
00:07:50,870 --> 00:07:56,070
So let's look at this from a very, very simplistic model.

168
00:07:56,070 --> 00:07:57,650
This is a one-dimensional model.

169
00:07:57,650 --> 00:08:00,670
It's a mapping of f of x, where x is a scalar.

170
00:08:00,670 --> 00:08:03,790
So here if we use, for example--

171
00:08:03,790 --> 00:08:05,130
these are true outputs.

172
00:08:05,130 --> 00:08:08,870
If we use a 5-layer ReLU-net, so essentially densely connected

173
00:08:08,870 --> 00:08:13,270
layers with point-wise ReLU-based activations.

174
00:08:13,270 --> 00:08:16,280
And we fit that to a single data point.

175
00:08:16,280 --> 00:08:18,030
Well, now the space of all possible models

176
00:08:18,030 --> 00:08:20,530
that fit that data point is very, very large.

177
00:08:20,530 --> 00:08:23,590
And the one that actually gets learned is just this flat line.

178
00:08:23,590 --> 00:08:26,950
So that's not necessarily a very good learned solution.

179
00:08:26,950 --> 00:08:29,190
As we start adding more data, you

180
00:08:29,190 --> 00:08:31,590
can see that you maybe start to fit slightly

181
00:08:31,590 --> 00:08:35,049
better in the region where we add data in the middle,

182
00:08:35,049 --> 00:08:36,850
but it's still not a very good solution.

183
00:08:36,850 --> 00:08:38,887
And then as you start to add a lot of data, what

184
00:08:38,887 --> 00:08:40,929
you see on the right is you start getting really,

185
00:08:40,929 --> 00:08:43,510
really nice fits in the distribution where

186
00:08:43,510 --> 00:08:46,630
you have data, but you get really poor generalization

187
00:08:46,630 --> 00:08:48,210
out of distribution.

188
00:08:48,210 --> 00:08:54,825
And so the intuition here is really that model, hypothesis,

189
00:08:54,825 --> 00:08:56,450
that hypothesis space you build, that's

190
00:08:56,450 --> 00:08:58,650
really something that helps us generalize out

191
00:08:58,650 --> 00:09:01,010
of the distribution of the data we have.

192
00:09:01,010 --> 00:09:04,570
And so what if instead we had a really strong

193
00:09:04,570 --> 00:09:07,890
prior over what the model was supposed to be?

194
00:09:07,890 --> 00:09:11,710
So in this case, y equals ax plus sine bx squared.

195
00:09:11,710 --> 00:09:13,510
That's very specific.

196
00:09:13,510 --> 00:09:16,730
It turns out that it's exactly the correct model

197
00:09:16,730 --> 00:09:18,370
for this function.

198
00:09:18,370 --> 00:09:20,850
And you can see that if you only have a single data point,

199
00:09:20,850 --> 00:09:23,512
that really good model is insufficient.

200
00:09:23,512 --> 00:09:24,970
You still aren't able to figure out

201
00:09:24,970 --> 00:09:30,450
where in that set of hypotheses the correct model is.

202
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:33,110
But even with very, very, very few data points,

203
00:09:33,110 --> 00:09:35,050
we start getting a really nice fit.

204
00:09:35,050 --> 00:09:37,752
And, actually, even when we add a lot more data points,

205
00:09:37,752 --> 00:09:39,210
we get really strong generalization

206
00:09:39,210 --> 00:09:40,030
out of distribution.

207
00:09:40,030 --> 00:09:41,410
But the difference between those two in the middle

208
00:09:41,410 --> 00:09:42,970
is actually not that big.

209
00:09:42,970 --> 00:09:45,890
So we can get a really nice fit even with just a little bit

210
00:09:45,890 --> 00:09:52,950
of data when we have the exact precise correct hypothesis.

211
00:09:52,950 --> 00:09:54,450
And so that's what we're showing.

212
00:09:54,450 --> 00:09:56,990
Architectures enable us to generalize outside the training

213
00:09:56,990 --> 00:09:58,230
distribution.

214
00:09:58,230 --> 00:09:59,950
And a good architecture is going to be

215
00:09:59,950 --> 00:10:03,650
one that can represent the true function of the data

216
00:10:03,650 --> 00:10:05,250
and then is otherwise minimal.

217
00:10:05,250 --> 00:10:07,350
We want it to be easy to search over

218
00:10:07,350 --> 00:10:09,030
via gradient-based learning.

219
00:10:09,030 --> 00:10:12,430
We want it to be easy to parallelize, fast on GPU,

220
00:10:12,430 --> 00:10:14,190
et cetera, et cetera.

221
00:10:14,190 --> 00:10:16,390
And so here's a bit of a preview.

222
00:10:16,390 --> 00:10:18,230
But it turns out that better architectures

223
00:10:18,230 --> 00:10:20,190
can approximate important function

224
00:10:20,190 --> 00:10:21,710
classes more efficiently.

225
00:10:21,710 --> 00:10:27,410
So this is a result from a paper by another MIT professor,

226
00:10:27,410 --> 00:10:31,150
Vincent Sitzmann, where they introduced this model over here

227
00:10:31,150 --> 00:10:33,990
on the far right called SIREN.

228
00:10:33,990 --> 00:10:39,030
And SIREN actually uses a sinusoidal activation space,

229
00:10:39,030 --> 00:10:41,410
and that fits images more efficiently.

230
00:10:41,410 --> 00:10:43,210
If anyone's taken signal processing,

231
00:10:43,210 --> 00:10:45,170
this idea might be a bit familiar.

232
00:10:45,170 --> 00:10:47,870
A Fourier basis is a good basis for representing images,

233
00:10:47,870 --> 00:10:50,650
and the fundamental building blocks of Fourier basis

234
00:10:50,650 --> 00:10:52,290
are sinusoids.

235
00:10:52,290 --> 00:10:54,930
So if we take a look at what this is, if you look at ReLU-net

236
00:10:54,930 --> 00:10:57,130
or a TanH-net or these different models

237
00:10:57,130 --> 00:10:58,470
that are being compared here.

238
00:10:58,470 --> 00:11:00,303
And we're going to look at how they actually

239
00:11:00,303 --> 00:11:02,650
try to fit this specific image over time

240
00:11:02,650 --> 00:11:04,450
by sampling pixels from that image

241
00:11:04,450 --> 00:11:07,890
and then fitting that as the training data.

242
00:11:07,890 --> 00:11:09,610
You can see that you start getting

243
00:11:09,610 --> 00:11:12,850
a pretty good approximation from SIREN

244
00:11:12,850 --> 00:11:16,010
much, much faster than you do from some of these other models.

245
00:11:16,010 --> 00:11:19,530
And, of course, after sampling enough points,

246
00:11:19,530 --> 00:11:22,090
eventually, all the models are getting

247
00:11:22,090 --> 00:11:24,070
reasonably good at estimating.

248
00:11:24,070 --> 00:11:26,850
But that SIREN model, it's a better hypothesis

249
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:28,390
to start with for the image space.

250
00:11:28,390 --> 00:11:31,690
And so you get much more efficient convergence.

251
00:11:31,690 --> 00:11:34,270
And so in this particular instance,

252
00:11:34,270 --> 00:11:36,950
now instead of looking at a 5-layer ReLU-net,

253
00:11:36,950 --> 00:11:39,370
we're going to look at a 5-layer sin-net where now those

254
00:11:39,370 --> 00:11:42,690
activations, those nonlinear activations, are sinusoids.

255
00:11:42,690 --> 00:11:45,450
And you can see here that with a single data point,

256
00:11:45,450 --> 00:11:47,590
it still doesn't fit super well.

257
00:11:47,590 --> 00:11:49,870
But even just with a couple data points,

258
00:11:49,870 --> 00:11:52,510
we start seeing some periodicity that

259
00:11:52,510 --> 00:11:54,970
maybe starts to match the periodicity in the function.

260
00:11:54,970 --> 00:11:56,610
And even though we're not generalizing perfectly,

261
00:11:56,610 --> 00:11:58,230
we're still seeing some periodicity

262
00:11:58,230 --> 00:12:00,990
that seems to be moving more towards what

263
00:12:00,990 --> 00:12:02,130
the true function is.

264
00:12:02,130 --> 00:12:06,470
So why would you have that periodicity in the output space?

265
00:12:06,470 --> 00:12:08,010
It's kind of an obvious question.

266
00:12:08,010 --> 00:12:09,522
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] sine.

267
00:12:09,522 --> 00:12:10,730
SARA BEERY: Yes, it's a sine.

268
00:12:10,730 --> 00:12:12,190
Sines are periodic.

269
00:12:12,190 --> 00:12:14,310
Very nice.

270
00:12:14,310 --> 00:12:17,870
But so essentially, we've just added an inductive bias

271
00:12:17,870 --> 00:12:20,310
in our model architecture that says that the distribution

272
00:12:20,310 --> 00:12:21,732
should be periodic.

273
00:12:21,732 --> 00:12:23,190
And then it tries to find solutions

274
00:12:23,190 --> 00:12:26,390
that are periodic outside of the distribution where

275
00:12:26,390 --> 00:12:27,950
we have training data.

276
00:12:27,950 --> 00:12:28,450
Cool.

277
00:12:28,450 --> 00:12:30,950
Any questions about this?

278
00:12:30,950 --> 00:12:33,450
It's really just intended to build high-level intuition.

279
00:12:33,450 --> 00:12:33,950
Yeah?

280
00:12:33,950 --> 00:12:36,430
AUDIENCE: So I guess I'm wondering how constrained

281
00:12:36,430 --> 00:12:40,430
is this to the number of pixels because the sinusoid period

282
00:12:40,430 --> 00:12:42,468
seems like it would be affected by that one.

283
00:12:42,468 --> 00:12:44,760
SARA BEERY: So one of the things with the Fourier basis

284
00:12:44,760 --> 00:12:47,960
is that you are building out--

285
00:12:47,960 --> 00:12:51,440
the basis assumes different periodicities of sine.

286
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:53,400
And so you're reconstructing the image

287
00:12:53,400 --> 00:12:55,200
using different periodicities and weights

288
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:56,700
of the sinusoid function.

289
00:12:56,700 --> 00:12:58,862
So that it handles some of the structure well.

290
00:12:58,862 --> 00:13:00,320
And it's one of the reasons that we

291
00:13:00,320 --> 00:13:02,112
can compress images really well with things

292
00:13:02,112 --> 00:13:04,480
like the fast Fourier transform.

293
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:06,166
Yeah.

294
00:13:06,166 --> 00:13:07,060
Cool.

295
00:13:07,060 --> 00:13:11,920
So let's now talk about convolutional neural networks.

296
00:13:11,920 --> 00:13:15,320
So here's an image.

297
00:13:15,320 --> 00:13:17,376
What's in this image?

298
00:13:17,376 --> 00:13:18,744
AUDIENCE: Birds.

299
00:13:18,744 --> 00:13:20,940
SARA BEERY: Birds, OK.

300
00:13:20,940 --> 00:13:24,400
So what else is in this image?

301
00:13:24,400 --> 00:13:25,200
AUDIENCE: Sky.

302
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:26,720
SARA BEERY: Sky.

303
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:29,407
So if I had a whole image classifier, if I took this image

304
00:13:29,407 --> 00:13:31,240
and I ran it through one of the models we've

305
00:13:31,240 --> 00:13:33,820
talked about so far, what would we want it to say?

306
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:39,666
What's the category of this image?

307
00:13:39,666 --> 00:13:41,237
AUDIENCE: Birds and sky.

308
00:13:41,237 --> 00:13:42,320
SARA BEERY: Birds and sky.

309
00:13:42,320 --> 00:13:43,445
I mean, that would be nice.

310
00:13:43,445 --> 00:13:45,820
We haven't talked about multi-output classification,

311
00:13:45,820 --> 00:13:46,320
though.

312
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:46,820
Yeah?

313
00:13:46,820 --> 00:13:47,640
AUDIENCE: Nature.

314
00:13:47,640 --> 00:13:50,660
SARA BEERY: Nature, yeah.

315
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:51,543
I love that answer.

316
00:13:51,543 --> 00:13:53,460
So one of the things that's really interesting

317
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:55,940
is you can really see this trade-off between generality

318
00:13:55,940 --> 00:13:57,540
and specificity.

319
00:13:57,540 --> 00:13:59,880
And so, yeah, it's like, oh, well, that's too hard.

320
00:13:59,880 --> 00:14:03,460
I'm just going to predict something coarser grained.

321
00:14:03,460 --> 00:14:06,640
And that's a reasonable fallback.

322
00:14:06,640 --> 00:14:10,300
So what I was trying to get at here is actually

323
00:14:10,300 --> 00:14:14,180
for a lot of things we might want to understand in an image,

324
00:14:14,180 --> 00:14:17,640
maybe a single category isn't sufficient information.

325
00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:20,520
This could be flock of birds, for example.

326
00:14:20,520 --> 00:14:22,980
I mean, maybe that's a category.

327
00:14:22,980 --> 00:14:25,620
But really, it's just that a single label is usually

328
00:14:25,620 --> 00:14:29,340
insufficient to represent the semantic concepts that

329
00:14:29,340 --> 00:14:30,400
are captured in images.

330
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:32,900
And this is actually, in the grand scheme of things,

331
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:34,000
a pretty simple image.

332
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:37,080
I mean, think about this versus an image like of Times Square,

333
00:14:37,080 --> 00:14:39,960
for example, how complex that image signals semantically

334
00:14:39,960 --> 00:14:41,400
could be.

335
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:44,687
So if you were to take a picture, for example, from where

336
00:14:44,687 --> 00:14:47,020
you're sitting, I don't know, out there in the audience,

337
00:14:47,020 --> 00:14:49,280
think about what would you categorize that picture?

338
00:14:49,280 --> 00:14:50,740
Is it lecture hall?

339
00:14:50,740 --> 00:14:51,700
Is that students?

340
00:14:51,700 --> 00:14:53,400
Is it projector?

341
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:54,300
Is it MIT?

342
00:14:54,300 --> 00:14:56,560
It's all those things.

343
00:14:56,560 --> 00:14:58,920
But so when we start to think about these more

344
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:01,920
complex semantic signals, and we'll

345
00:15:01,920 --> 00:15:05,400
get into a lot more of this throughout the course,

346
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:07,880
one thing that starts to be more and more true

347
00:15:07,880 --> 00:15:14,320
is that there's often maybe different categories

348
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:17,440
or different bits of information that are somewhat locally

349
00:15:17,440 --> 00:15:18,233
constrained.

350
00:15:18,233 --> 00:15:20,400
And so you can think about breaking down the problem

351
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:22,317
into something that actually looks at location

352
00:15:22,317 --> 00:15:24,680
as well as semantics.

353
00:15:24,680 --> 00:15:26,560
So one way that we could try to address

354
00:15:26,560 --> 00:15:29,880
the fact that this image contains multiple things is we

355
00:15:29,880 --> 00:15:32,327
could first subdivide the image into patches.

356
00:15:32,327 --> 00:15:34,660
And then we could try to classify each patch separately.

357
00:15:34,660 --> 00:15:38,800
So you could take this image, chop it up into little pieces,

358
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:40,840
and then maybe you have a classifier.

359
00:15:40,840 --> 00:15:44,620
And now you send this patch to the classifier and it says bird.

360
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:47,960
And then you could run that classifier over the entire grid.

361
00:15:47,960 --> 00:15:50,140
And you would take all those patches independently.

362
00:15:50,140 --> 00:15:51,580
And you could then--

363
00:15:51,580 --> 00:15:52,360
hey, bird.

364
00:15:52,360 --> 00:15:54,460
This one is sky.

365
00:15:54,460 --> 00:15:59,220
And you could densely populate that entire space

366
00:15:59,220 --> 00:16:04,380
and get out the category per little block or little patch.

367
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:06,780
And so if we think about this and we

368
00:16:06,780 --> 00:16:08,820
have some function that's going to crop this up

369
00:16:08,820 --> 00:16:13,960
and map every patch to a category, in some way,

370
00:16:13,960 --> 00:16:17,600
this is actually giving us an image output.

371
00:16:17,600 --> 00:16:19,520
It's a bit like a low-resolution image.

372
00:16:19,520 --> 00:16:23,540
It's got some spatial context, which is useful.

373
00:16:23,540 --> 00:16:26,460
But there are a couple patches here

374
00:16:26,460 --> 00:16:28,420
where the objects don't necessarily

375
00:16:28,420 --> 00:16:30,660
fit super neatly inside them.

376
00:16:30,660 --> 00:16:33,500
So I don't know, maybe this one over here

377
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:36,240
that's got like the tip of the bird's wing

378
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:38,400
just in that one edge of the patch.

379
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:40,260
It gets a little ambiguous.

380
00:16:40,260 --> 00:16:42,428
Do you want that to be that patch,

381
00:16:42,428 --> 00:16:44,220
that whole patch to be categorized as bird,

382
00:16:44,220 --> 00:16:45,460
if there's any bird at all?

383
00:16:45,460 --> 00:16:47,252
Do you want it to be-- what is the majority

384
00:16:47,252 --> 00:16:49,480
of the pixels in the patch?

385
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:52,660
And so maybe we want a higher resolution output map.

386
00:16:52,660 --> 00:16:55,400
We don't want to just know at this really coarse resolution.

387
00:16:55,400 --> 00:16:59,320
We actually want a finer resolution of the output.

388
00:16:59,320 --> 00:17:00,880
So one way you could do that is you

389
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:03,160
could use smaller and smaller patches.

390
00:17:03,160 --> 00:17:05,079
But it turns out that as the patches get

391
00:17:05,079 --> 00:17:09,440
smaller and at the far extreme, the patches are a single pixel.

392
00:17:09,440 --> 00:17:11,680
From a single pixel, you don't have enough context

393
00:17:11,680 --> 00:17:14,880
to determine what the semantic category is.

394
00:17:14,880 --> 00:17:16,800
It's not easy to recognize content

395
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:19,280
as those patches get small.

396
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:24,520
So instead, the winning idea here

397
00:17:24,520 --> 00:17:25,960
with convolutional neural networks

398
00:17:25,960 --> 00:17:31,840
is we use large but overlapping patches.

399
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:35,860
The very high-level intuition behind all of this is that you

400
00:17:35,860 --> 00:17:39,420
want context that's local to be--

401
00:17:39,420 --> 00:17:41,100
you want to be able to look around

402
00:17:41,100 --> 00:17:44,300
the pixel you're predicting at when you make a prediction,

403
00:17:44,300 --> 00:17:48,460
but you also want to do this in a dense way.

404
00:17:48,460 --> 00:17:52,463
So the way that would look is, OK, you have your patch size,

405
00:17:52,463 --> 00:17:54,880
and that patch size is probably a parameter in your model.

406
00:17:54,880 --> 00:17:56,420
You want to make sure that context window is

407
00:17:56,420 --> 00:17:57,795
kind of big enough that you maybe

408
00:17:57,795 --> 00:18:00,380
see the whole object of bird.

409
00:18:00,380 --> 00:18:02,280
But then when you're actually predicting,

410
00:18:02,280 --> 00:18:05,940
you're sliding this over as you go.

411
00:18:05,940 --> 00:18:08,980
And then maybe you're only predicting, for example,

412
00:18:08,980 --> 00:18:12,140
the object class of the center pixel but given the context

413
00:18:12,140 --> 00:18:15,100
around that pixel.

414
00:18:15,100 --> 00:18:19,140
And so now your training data would be that patch and then

415
00:18:19,140 --> 00:18:23,540
the output category of that center pixel, for example.

416
00:18:23,540 --> 00:18:27,380
And now, if you actually run this categorizer

417
00:18:27,380 --> 00:18:29,620
over your entire image, you start

418
00:18:29,620 --> 00:18:33,320
to get something that actually looks pretty cool.

419
00:18:33,320 --> 00:18:37,600
That starts to look like, semantically, the boundaries

420
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:39,980
of one category and another.

421
00:18:39,980 --> 00:18:42,960
And this problem of categorizing every pixel

422
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:44,760
instead of categorizing a whole image

423
00:18:44,760 --> 00:18:46,960
is called semantic segmentation.

424
00:18:46,960 --> 00:18:50,040
And here we're categorizing every pixel as one a set

425
00:18:50,040 --> 00:18:52,200
of categories.

426
00:18:52,200 --> 00:18:53,420
This can be extended.

427
00:18:53,420 --> 00:18:57,255
And maybe we'll talk a bit later about instance segmentation,

428
00:18:57,255 --> 00:18:58,380
which is a similar problem.

429
00:18:58,380 --> 00:19:01,320
But now you want to identify unique instances of a given

430
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:04,520
category and capture them separately.

431
00:19:04,520 --> 00:19:09,800
So now you have essentially the building blocks

432
00:19:09,800 --> 00:19:13,240
of a convolutional neural network.

433
00:19:13,240 --> 00:19:15,800
And one thing that's nice about this

434
00:19:15,800 --> 00:19:18,920
is because you're processing each of these patches

435
00:19:18,920 --> 00:19:21,520
independently and identically, that

436
00:19:21,520 --> 00:19:25,760
means that the actual model itself is

437
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:28,880
invariant to translations of the input.

438
00:19:28,880 --> 00:19:32,440
So this is an equivariant mapping essentially.

439
00:19:32,440 --> 00:19:34,660
So since you're processing every single patch

440
00:19:34,660 --> 00:19:38,500
in the same way, that means that it doesn't matter if the bird is

441
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:42,260
in the top corner, the center, the bottom left, those pixels

442
00:19:42,260 --> 00:19:44,940
are still going to be categorized as bird.

443
00:19:44,940 --> 00:19:46,460
And another way to think about this

444
00:19:46,460 --> 00:19:49,940
is if you took your function and you applied it

445
00:19:49,940 --> 00:19:51,900
to a translation of the input, it

446
00:19:51,900 --> 00:19:55,780
would be the same as if you translated a function

447
00:19:55,780 --> 00:19:57,660
of the original input.

448
00:19:57,660 --> 00:20:00,340
And why would this maybe be a nice thing

449
00:20:00,340 --> 00:20:03,740
to have for something like semantic segmentation?

450
00:20:03,740 --> 00:20:07,540
Why does this make sense as an inductive bias or a hypothesis

451
00:20:07,540 --> 00:20:12,290
for images and for categorizing like this?

452
00:20:12,290 --> 00:20:13,345
Yeah?

453
00:20:13,345 --> 00:20:15,220
AUDIENCE: Like the answer for each pixel kind

454
00:20:15,220 --> 00:20:18,120
of corresponds to the [INAUDIBLE] next slide

455
00:20:18,120 --> 00:20:20,052
[INAUDIBLE].

456
00:20:20,052 --> 00:20:21,260
SARA BEERY: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

457
00:20:21,260 --> 00:20:27,140
So the answer corresponds to that local context window.

458
00:20:27,140 --> 00:20:29,660
Yeah, if you took an image of a bird

459
00:20:29,660 --> 00:20:32,760
and then you moved that portion of the image corresponding

460
00:20:32,760 --> 00:20:35,360
to a bird to a different part of the image or imagine

461
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:37,480
you were actually taking a photo and then you

462
00:20:37,480 --> 00:20:39,140
shifted your camera a little bit,

463
00:20:39,140 --> 00:20:42,080
the object you're taking a photo of is the same.

464
00:20:42,080 --> 00:20:46,920
So there actually is this type of symmetry innate to categories

465
00:20:46,920 --> 00:20:48,020
in the real world.

466
00:20:48,020 --> 00:20:49,520
And this symmetry is something we've

467
00:20:49,520 --> 00:20:53,000
built into our hypothesis space via the model structure.

468
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:57,040
And so, yeah, the world is somewhat translation invariant.

469
00:20:57,040 --> 00:21:00,345
A bird should look the same no matter where it is in an image.

470
00:21:00,345 --> 00:21:01,720
Of course, we're kind of glossing

471
00:21:01,720 --> 00:21:06,140
over pose, which is a more complicated factor here.

472
00:21:06,140 --> 00:21:07,920
But if you have something at the same pose

473
00:21:07,920 --> 00:21:09,840
and you move it somewhere else, it still

474
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:12,880
maintains the same category.

475
00:21:12,880 --> 00:21:15,020
So cool.

476
00:21:15,020 --> 00:21:19,720
One possible version of this, of this model,

477
00:21:19,720 --> 00:21:24,240
would be to compute a weighted sum of the pixels in each patch.

478
00:21:24,240 --> 00:21:27,000
And then the weights of those that sum, that's

479
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:28,260
what we're going to learn.

480
00:21:28,260 --> 00:21:32,250
And so if you do this, then essentially

481
00:21:32,250 --> 00:21:37,210
that set of linear weights, W, is a convolutional kernel

482
00:21:37,210 --> 00:21:41,633
that then we can apply to the entire image.

483
00:21:41,633 --> 00:21:44,050
And so what does this mean/ What does this kind of concept

484
00:21:44,050 --> 00:21:47,730
of convolution and a filter or a kernel?

485
00:21:47,730 --> 00:21:52,010
So a convolution is a linear, shift-invariant transformation

486
00:21:52,010 --> 00:21:56,130
of grid-structured input data.

487
00:21:56,130 --> 00:21:58,210
And essentially, in signal processing,

488
00:21:58,210 --> 00:22:00,017
convolution is a way that you do filtering.

489
00:22:00,017 --> 00:22:01,850
So how many of you have taken, I don't know,

490
00:22:01,850 --> 00:22:04,770
a signal processing class or learned signal processing

491
00:22:04,770 --> 00:22:06,710
maybe in like an electrical engineering class?

492
00:22:06,710 --> 00:22:08,210
Raise hands.

493
00:22:08,210 --> 00:22:12,730
Cool, so a good number of you have seen this before.

494
00:22:12,730 --> 00:22:15,410
And here, the filter or the weights

495
00:22:15,410 --> 00:22:19,027
we use to take that linear weighted sum.

496
00:22:19,027 --> 00:22:21,610
And then mathematically, if we want to structure that out here

497
00:22:21,610 --> 00:22:25,515
at the bottom, the output for a given location

498
00:22:25,515 --> 00:22:26,890
when applying the filter is going

499
00:22:26,890 --> 00:22:31,190
to be some bias, plus a linear combination of the filter weight

500
00:22:31,190 --> 00:22:33,830
for that coordinate times the pixel value for that

501
00:22:33,830 --> 00:22:35,390
coordinate in the image, and then

502
00:22:35,390 --> 00:22:37,598
summed over all the filter weights and all the pixels

503
00:22:37,598 --> 00:22:38,630
in the patch.

504
00:22:38,630 --> 00:22:41,750
So really what it's doing is for any given

505
00:22:41,750 --> 00:22:43,590
filter-- like in this case, this filter

506
00:22:43,590 --> 00:22:46,550
is kind of diffused all around, and then you

507
00:22:46,550 --> 00:22:49,870
have something dark and something light.

508
00:22:49,870 --> 00:22:53,750
So this filter is going to look for that pattern.

509
00:22:53,750 --> 00:22:56,610
By look for, that's very anthropomorphized.

510
00:22:56,610 --> 00:22:59,710
But you're going to produce maximum output

511
00:22:59,710 --> 00:23:01,950
values when you have that same kind

512
00:23:01,950 --> 00:23:03,868
of dark to light transition.

513
00:23:03,868 --> 00:23:05,410
And so what that actually looks like,

514
00:23:05,410 --> 00:23:08,630
if you run that filter over this image,

515
00:23:08,630 --> 00:23:11,510
is you start to get something that's highlighting areas

516
00:23:11,510 --> 00:23:16,550
in the image that match the filter the most, so

517
00:23:16,550 --> 00:23:17,970
match it most closely.

518
00:23:17,970 --> 00:23:21,070
So here we can see that we're getting really high values, so

519
00:23:21,070 --> 00:23:23,950
the white values along the boundaries between dark

520
00:23:23,950 --> 00:23:24,710
to light.

521
00:23:24,710 --> 00:23:26,810
And we're actually getting really low values

522
00:23:26,810 --> 00:23:28,830
along the boundaries from light to dark

523
00:23:28,830 --> 00:23:31,050
because that's the place that's going to be maximally

524
00:23:31,050 --> 00:23:34,330
opposite to that filter.

525
00:23:34,330 --> 00:23:36,810
So in this particular case, what we've got

526
00:23:36,810 --> 00:23:39,770
is a filter that's finding us horizontal edges

527
00:23:39,770 --> 00:23:42,810
between dark and light values.

528
00:23:42,810 --> 00:23:43,590
Any questions?

529
00:23:43,590 --> 00:23:44,690
Yeah?

530
00:23:44,690 --> 00:23:46,330
AUDIENCE: Can you explain [INAUDIBLE]

531
00:23:46,330 --> 00:23:48,662
variance transformation in this context?

532
00:23:48,662 --> 00:23:49,370
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

533
00:23:49,370 --> 00:23:55,570
So, again, here if I took this image of a clownfish

534
00:23:55,570 --> 00:24:00,770
and I shifted it over and I ran this convolutional filter

535
00:24:00,770 --> 00:24:04,810
over it, the output would be this shifted over.

536
00:24:04,810 --> 00:24:07,050
And so you could shift the input,

537
00:24:07,050 --> 00:24:08,353
then apply the convolution.

538
00:24:08,353 --> 00:24:09,770
Or you could apply the convolution

539
00:24:09,770 --> 00:24:12,312
and then shift the output, and the results would be the same.

540
00:24:15,330 --> 00:24:19,610
So now let's look at this from a more neural networky

541
00:24:19,610 --> 00:24:21,170
perspective.

542
00:24:21,170 --> 00:24:22,910
So this is a fully connected layer.

543
00:24:22,910 --> 00:24:24,750
This is what we've seen so far.

544
00:24:24,750 --> 00:24:29,430
And here all the outputs depend on all of the inputs.

545
00:24:29,430 --> 00:24:31,510
And this can be quite computationally expensive

546
00:24:31,510 --> 00:24:33,750
when things get very large.

547
00:24:33,750 --> 00:24:36,790
Instead of a fully connected network,

548
00:24:36,790 --> 00:24:40,470
a convolutional network is essentially a locally connected

549
00:24:40,470 --> 00:24:41,630
network.

550
00:24:41,630 --> 00:24:45,950
And so often we can assume that the output then of this model

551
00:24:45,950 --> 00:24:48,950
is a local function of the input.

552
00:24:48,950 --> 00:24:52,270
So here if we use the same weights

553
00:24:52,270 --> 00:24:56,870
for these linear combinations, this w matrix,

554
00:24:56,870 --> 00:24:59,230
if we use those weights the same regardless

555
00:24:59,230 --> 00:25:04,870
of where that initial input values are coming from,

556
00:25:04,870 --> 00:25:08,170
that gives us a convolutional neural network.

557
00:25:08,170 --> 00:25:10,310
And the way that we actually denote that

558
00:25:10,310 --> 00:25:13,470
is with this star operation for convolution.

559
00:25:13,470 --> 00:25:15,470
If you have take signal processing,

560
00:25:15,470 --> 00:25:17,830
actually, this is not quite the same definition

561
00:25:17,830 --> 00:25:20,490
as familiarly used in signal processing for convolution.

562
00:25:20,490 --> 00:25:23,970
Usually, it has like a flipped value.

563
00:25:23,970 --> 00:25:26,950
But here when we talk about neural networks,

564
00:25:26,950 --> 00:25:29,337
instead of calling this cross correlation, which

565
00:25:29,337 --> 00:25:31,670
might be what it is in the signal processing literature,

566
00:25:31,670 --> 00:25:33,227
we just call this convolution.

567
00:25:33,227 --> 00:25:35,310
One of those reasons is because in these networks,

568
00:25:35,310 --> 00:25:37,190
it's very easy to learn how to flip a sign,

569
00:25:37,190 --> 00:25:38,430
so it doesn't matter so much.

570
00:25:38,430 --> 00:25:38,930
Yeah?

571
00:25:38,930 --> 00:25:41,170
AUDIENCE: Is it always a five-point star?

572
00:25:41,170 --> 00:25:44,250
SARA BEERY: I mean, people do all sorts of stuff.

573
00:25:44,250 --> 00:25:46,730
We are going to use a five-point star in our lectures

574
00:25:46,730 --> 00:25:49,010
and in the notes.

575
00:25:49,010 --> 00:25:52,090
I think it's kind of pretty.

576
00:25:52,090 --> 00:25:53,830
So what does this actually look like?

577
00:25:53,830 --> 00:25:57,350
So here we have this same linear combination of weights.

578
00:25:57,350 --> 00:26:00,202
We're running it over the local structure.

579
00:26:00,202 --> 00:26:01,910
And then you're taking that weighted sum.

580
00:26:01,910 --> 00:26:03,810
And so the outputs for each of these values

581
00:26:03,810 --> 00:26:06,845
are going to be corresponding to just that local input.

582
00:26:06,845 --> 00:26:08,970
But it's going to be the same weights that are used

583
00:26:08,970 --> 00:26:10,870
across the entire input vector.

584
00:26:13,450 --> 00:26:16,930
And the nice thing with that thing

585
00:26:16,930 --> 00:26:20,370
is that you get this, again, embarrassing parallelism

586
00:26:20,370 --> 00:26:26,750
because now you can apply that same weight matrix across only

587
00:26:26,750 --> 00:26:29,050
smaller patches of the input.

588
00:26:29,050 --> 00:26:30,790
But you can do that in parallel.

589
00:26:30,790 --> 00:26:34,870
And if you want to think about what

590
00:26:34,870 --> 00:26:39,150
that looks like in this broader sense, if you're taking a fully

591
00:26:39,150 --> 00:26:43,090
connected layer, that's the same as taking your input vector,

592
00:26:43,090 --> 00:26:46,390
multiplying it by a dense weight matrix, where

593
00:26:46,390 --> 00:26:49,110
every single one of those values matters.

594
00:26:49,110 --> 00:26:51,150
And then that's how you get your output.

595
00:26:51,150 --> 00:26:53,670
And now you could think about actually

596
00:26:53,670 --> 00:26:56,670
a convolutional layer is the same.

597
00:26:56,670 --> 00:27:00,190
But now the weight matrix has a very specific structure.

598
00:27:00,190 --> 00:27:02,770
So instead of being dense, it's going to be very sparse.

599
00:27:02,770 --> 00:27:04,728
So in this particular instance where we're only

600
00:27:04,728 --> 00:27:06,870
looking at a sliding window of maybe three

601
00:27:06,870 --> 00:27:10,910
elements of that input vector, those three elements

602
00:27:10,910 --> 00:27:14,530
are going to be the only-- above and below that diagonal,

603
00:27:14,530 --> 00:27:17,167
those are going to be the only components that have values,

604
00:27:17,167 --> 00:27:18,750
and the values are going to be exactly

605
00:27:18,750 --> 00:27:22,330
the same along the entire diagonal of that matrix.

606
00:27:22,330 --> 00:27:24,950
And there's a special name for this type of matrix.

607
00:27:24,950 --> 00:27:28,890
It's a specific form of a Toeplitz matrix.

608
00:27:28,890 --> 00:27:30,490
And so one of the things that's cool

609
00:27:30,490 --> 00:27:33,650
here is that it's essentially a constraint

610
00:27:33,650 --> 00:27:35,810
on a standard linear layer.

611
00:27:35,810 --> 00:27:37,633
And there are fewer parameters that

612
00:27:37,633 --> 00:27:39,050
can be learned because you're just

613
00:27:39,050 --> 00:27:43,070
learning essentially parameters for just that local structure.

614
00:27:43,070 --> 00:27:45,670
So there's less of them, and they're going to be repeated.

615
00:27:45,670 --> 00:27:48,770
And so that means you can have a lot less overfitting.

616
00:27:48,770 --> 00:27:51,533
There's less open flexibility.

617
00:27:51,533 --> 00:27:53,950
And you're building some nice constraints into the system.

618
00:27:53,950 --> 00:27:55,190
You're making it equivariant.

619
00:27:55,190 --> 00:27:57,850
You're making it translation invariant.

620
00:27:57,850 --> 00:28:00,330
And then another nice benefit of this

621
00:28:00,330 --> 00:28:02,970
is because this thing has this specific structure where

622
00:28:02,970 --> 00:28:04,790
you have zeros almost everywhere,

623
00:28:04,790 --> 00:28:09,090
except for just above and below the diagonal,

624
00:28:09,090 --> 00:28:12,970
this can generalize to different input sizes

625
00:28:12,970 --> 00:28:16,190
because you can take that same weight matrix

626
00:28:16,190 --> 00:28:17,230
and you can scale it.

627
00:28:17,230 --> 00:28:18,630
It's not going to change anything

628
00:28:18,630 --> 00:28:20,230
about the model you've learned.

629
00:28:20,230 --> 00:28:22,390
And so that means that convolutional layers

630
00:28:22,390 --> 00:28:24,390
can be applied to arbitrarily sized

631
00:28:24,390 --> 00:28:26,670
inputs, which generalizes beyond the training

632
00:28:26,670 --> 00:28:28,650
data in a really nice way.

633
00:28:28,650 --> 00:28:31,350
Whereas if you took an MLP and you applied it

634
00:28:31,350 --> 00:28:34,150
to an image and then you tried to apply it to a larger image,

635
00:28:34,150 --> 00:28:35,610
you couldn't do it.

636
00:28:35,610 --> 00:28:38,330
This isn't something that you could apply to a larger image.

637
00:28:38,330 --> 00:28:40,950
You just wouldn't have weights for some of that size.

638
00:28:40,950 --> 00:28:42,397
And so one way to look at this is

639
00:28:42,397 --> 00:28:44,230
like you could take that same filter that we

640
00:28:44,230 --> 00:28:46,970
applied to the clownfish, and you could apply it to any image.

641
00:28:46,970 --> 00:28:49,830
And you would always get out these edges,

642
00:28:49,830 --> 00:28:51,990
these specific things.

643
00:28:51,990 --> 00:28:56,590
So five kind of high-level views-- yeah?

644
00:28:56,590 --> 00:28:58,370
AUDIENCE: If you go back just like before.

645
00:28:58,370 --> 00:28:59,230
Yeah.

646
00:28:59,230 --> 00:29:03,390
If you scale the matrix, does each pixel color

647
00:29:03,390 --> 00:29:05,430
here correspond to one value?

648
00:29:05,430 --> 00:29:09,630
SARA BEERY: So we talked about the weights,

649
00:29:09,630 --> 00:29:12,770
so w1, w0, w minus 1.

650
00:29:12,770 --> 00:29:16,330
So that's like if you're taking this from a vector perspective,

651
00:29:16,330 --> 00:29:18,890
you want to look at the wait just before and just after.

652
00:29:18,890 --> 00:29:22,610
That means that you're only going to have three values.

653
00:29:22,610 --> 00:29:24,610
And those three values, if you transfer this

654
00:29:24,610 --> 00:29:26,330
into a linear layer, those are going

655
00:29:26,330 --> 00:29:29,530
to be the values above, along, and below the diagonal

656
00:29:29,530 --> 00:29:30,430
for the whole matrix.

657
00:29:30,430 --> 00:29:32,930
So you just take that and slide like the ladder

658
00:29:32,930 --> 00:29:34,150
out along the diagonal.

659
00:29:34,150 --> 00:29:37,410
It'd be the same, like that.

660
00:29:41,050 --> 00:29:41,570
Cool.

661
00:29:41,570 --> 00:29:45,070
So some different ways to think about convolution.

662
00:29:45,070 --> 00:29:49,230
So, one, convolutional layers are equivariant to translation.

663
00:29:49,230 --> 00:29:51,430
So this is a built-in hypothesis.

664
00:29:51,430 --> 00:29:55,430
Two, convolutional layers enable patch processing.

665
00:29:55,430 --> 00:29:58,050
It builds in explicit parallelism

666
00:29:58,050 --> 00:29:59,390
into our computation.

667
00:29:59,390 --> 00:30:02,330
It makes it computationally efficient.

668
00:30:02,330 --> 00:30:04,090
Three, convolutional layers can be

669
00:30:04,090 --> 00:30:06,410
thought of as kind of image filters, things

670
00:30:06,410 --> 00:30:10,090
that are highlighting specific structures in the input data

671
00:30:10,090 --> 00:30:13,130
in some useful way that's learned.

672
00:30:13,130 --> 00:30:16,420
Or you can think of them as parameter sharing.

673
00:30:16,420 --> 00:30:19,340
You're using the same parameters across the entire input

674
00:30:19,340 --> 00:30:22,620
data in a computationally efficient way.

675
00:30:22,620 --> 00:30:25,140
And the last point is you can think of them as a way

676
00:30:25,140 --> 00:30:28,380
to process variable size tensors because you

677
00:30:28,380 --> 00:30:31,620
have this built-in generalization across the input

678
00:30:31,620 --> 00:30:33,040
size.

679
00:30:33,040 --> 00:30:35,660
Cool.

680
00:30:35,660 --> 00:30:39,140
So what happens when you stack convolutional layers?

681
00:30:39,140 --> 00:30:43,780
So here what we've got is two sets of convolutional layers.

682
00:30:43,780 --> 00:30:46,300
So, first, our input data comes in.

683
00:30:46,300 --> 00:30:48,300
We have a first convolutional layer.

684
00:30:48,300 --> 00:30:50,140
Then we have a non-linearity.

685
00:30:50,140 --> 00:30:52,540
And then we have a second convolutional layer.

686
00:30:52,540 --> 00:30:54,780
And it turns out that if you think of it this way,

687
00:30:54,780 --> 00:30:59,380
actually then the entire CNN is a convolutional filter, but one

688
00:30:59,380 --> 00:31:00,780
that's nonlinear.

689
00:31:00,780 --> 00:31:03,900
So you can really think of this way.

690
00:31:03,900 --> 00:31:06,980
And so here now this output depends only

691
00:31:06,980 --> 00:31:08,840
on the local structure of that input.

692
00:31:08,840 --> 00:31:11,580
And that function, even though it has that non-linearity

693
00:31:11,580 --> 00:31:15,380
in the middle, is the same for anywhere,

694
00:31:15,380 --> 00:31:17,440
any of these local patches in the input image.

695
00:31:17,440 --> 00:31:18,640
Does this make sense?

696
00:31:18,640 --> 00:31:19,220
Yeah?

697
00:31:19,220 --> 00:31:20,960
AUDIENCE: Why is it [INAUDIBLE]?

698
00:31:24,620 --> 00:31:25,360
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

699
00:31:25,360 --> 00:31:27,900
So I'm just saying here in this particular instance,

700
00:31:27,900 --> 00:31:30,920
assume now that you have a single convolutional layer,

701
00:31:30,920 --> 00:31:34,440
a point-wise non-linearity just like we had for MLP, and then

702
00:31:34,440 --> 00:31:37,120
another convolutional layer.

703
00:31:37,120 --> 00:31:39,080
So since we said that a convolutional layer is

704
00:31:39,080 --> 00:31:41,420
essentially a constrained version of a linear layer,

705
00:31:41,420 --> 00:31:43,520
you can essentially take it the same way.

706
00:31:43,520 --> 00:31:45,840
But the nice thing is that constrained version

707
00:31:45,840 --> 00:31:49,000
of the linear layer, it maintains that local constraint

708
00:31:49,000 --> 00:31:53,160
even as you start to stack layers.

709
00:31:53,160 --> 00:31:57,760
But one thing that's very clear is as you're adding more depth,

710
00:31:57,760 --> 00:31:59,800
you're increasing what we call the receptive

711
00:31:59,800 --> 00:32:01,480
field of the output.

712
00:32:01,480 --> 00:32:04,420
So you're adding more layers.

713
00:32:04,420 --> 00:32:06,960
That means that the local patch that

714
00:32:06,960 --> 00:32:09,480
corresponds to, for example, xL of j

715
00:32:09,480 --> 00:32:12,860
is larger than the local patch of a single one

716
00:32:12,860 --> 00:32:14,980
of those same layers because you're

717
00:32:14,980 --> 00:32:16,940
taking an input from a larger size

718
00:32:16,940 --> 00:32:18,400
and then you're doing that again.

719
00:32:18,400 --> 00:32:23,160
And this is what we call a spatial pyramid, for example.

720
00:32:23,160 --> 00:32:23,660
Yeah?

721
00:32:23,660 --> 00:32:26,090
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

722
00:32:26,090 --> 00:32:26,840
SARA BEERY: Sorry.

723
00:32:26,840 --> 00:32:27,965
Can you be a little louder?

724
00:32:27,965 --> 00:32:29,750
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

725
00:32:29,750 --> 00:32:30,860
SARA BEERY: Oh yeah, yeah.

726
00:32:30,860 --> 00:32:33,860
So the only reason this is nonlinear

727
00:32:33,860 --> 00:32:36,380
is because there's a non-linearity in the middle.

728
00:32:36,380 --> 00:32:39,100
So from between that first layer--

729
00:32:39,100 --> 00:32:41,220
so you have this first convolutional layer,

730
00:32:41,220 --> 00:32:43,660
and then see where you have those nodes in the middle.

731
00:32:43,660 --> 00:32:45,700
What I'm saying is here we've applied

732
00:32:45,700 --> 00:32:48,060
an activation, a nonlinear activation.

733
00:32:48,060 --> 00:32:49,560
And then you have that second layer.

734
00:32:49,560 --> 00:32:51,352
Maybe that's something that in future years

735
00:32:51,352 --> 00:32:53,040
I'll just put that in the diagram

736
00:32:53,040 --> 00:32:54,593
so you can see that non-linearity,

737
00:32:54,593 --> 00:32:55,760
so it'd be a little clearer.

738
00:32:55,760 --> 00:32:59,500
But I'm just saying it exists.

739
00:32:59,500 --> 00:33:01,440
So now you have a convolutional filter,

740
00:33:01,440 --> 00:33:04,180
but it's nonlinear because you have that nonlinear activation

741
00:33:04,180 --> 00:33:06,120
that you've put in the middle.

742
00:33:06,120 --> 00:33:06,620
Yeah?

743
00:33:06,620 --> 00:33:10,993
AUDIENCE: What is it called when there's [INAUDIBLE] established

744
00:33:10,993 --> 00:33:13,160
when you're pulling weights from previous dependents

745
00:33:13,160 --> 00:33:14,880
and previous layers [INAUDIBLE]?

746
00:33:14,880 --> 00:33:19,000
SARA BEERY: A receptive field is one term that people will use.

747
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:21,640
So if you're like increasing the receptive field of your model,

748
00:33:21,640 --> 00:33:23,140
you can do that in a couple of ways.

749
00:33:23,140 --> 00:33:25,400
You can make the original patch size,

750
00:33:25,400 --> 00:33:28,520
input size larger, so the kernel size of the filter size

751
00:33:28,520 --> 00:33:31,060
could get larger, or you can just add more depth.

752
00:33:31,060 --> 00:33:34,520
And that will also increase the receptive field.

753
00:33:34,520 --> 00:33:35,240
Yeah?

754
00:33:35,240 --> 00:33:37,040
AUDIENCE: A couple slides back, you

755
00:33:37,040 --> 00:33:40,840
represented acts as a vector.

756
00:33:40,840 --> 00:33:43,652
So that's an image that's been flattened into a vector.

757
00:33:43,652 --> 00:33:44,360
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

758
00:33:44,360 --> 00:33:46,068
So right now, we're talking about vectors

759
00:33:46,068 --> 00:33:48,232
because they're really clean and easy to look at.

760
00:33:48,232 --> 00:33:50,440
But, yeah, you can essentially say, you take an image

761
00:33:50,440 --> 00:33:52,533
and you flatten it out, though I think

762
00:33:52,533 --> 00:33:54,200
it's a little more complicated than that

763
00:33:54,200 --> 00:33:57,320
because you wouldn't take an image

764
00:33:57,320 --> 00:33:59,740
and flatten it out and then apply convolution like this

765
00:33:59,740 --> 00:34:02,120
because you would have this weird like stripe-wise

766
00:34:02,120 --> 00:34:02,700
structure.

767
00:34:02,700 --> 00:34:05,880
So, actually, we do take those convolutions in two dimensions.

768
00:34:05,880 --> 00:34:07,080
Yeah?

769
00:34:07,080 --> 00:34:10,675
AUDIENCE: In the second layer, would you use the same kernel?

770
00:34:10,675 --> 00:34:11,300
SARA BEERY: No.

771
00:34:11,300 --> 00:34:12,967
And that's why the colors are different.

772
00:34:12,967 --> 00:34:13,980
I mean, you could.

773
00:34:13,980 --> 00:34:15,475
But usually, we don't.

774
00:34:15,475 --> 00:34:17,100
Those weights are learned, but they are

775
00:34:17,100 --> 00:34:18,600
shared across the entire layer.

776
00:34:18,600 --> 00:34:19,968
Yeah?

777
00:34:19,968 --> 00:34:22,260
AUDIENCE: So just to make sure I understand [INAUDIBLE]

778
00:34:22,260 --> 00:34:27,260
each weight matrix in each layer [INAUDIBLE]

779
00:34:27,260 --> 00:34:38,380
except for the [INAUDIBLE] layers in our network in order

780
00:34:38,380 --> 00:34:41,778
to represent the [INAUDIBLE].

781
00:34:41,778 --> 00:34:44,320
SARA BEERY: So the question is like for each of these layers,

782
00:34:44,320 --> 00:34:46,260
now that we have a convolutional layer,

783
00:34:46,260 --> 00:34:49,340
they can be represented as that sparse matrix where you only

784
00:34:49,340 --> 00:34:51,219
are looking along the diagonal.

785
00:34:51,219 --> 00:34:54,980
We don't always necessarily actually compute it that way.

786
00:34:54,980 --> 00:34:56,800
For efficiency or for parallelism,

787
00:34:56,800 --> 00:34:59,580
often, we just really do compute it patch wise.

788
00:34:59,580 --> 00:35:03,820
But your point was if you're now really restricting the search

789
00:35:03,820 --> 00:35:07,980
space by only allowing like a few parameters per layer,

790
00:35:07,980 --> 00:35:10,920
doesn't that mean that you would really struggle

791
00:35:10,920 --> 00:35:13,640
to represent images or learn?

792
00:35:13,640 --> 00:35:16,980
And the answer is yes.

793
00:35:16,980 --> 00:35:19,233
And so that's why often with convolutional layers,

794
00:35:19,233 --> 00:35:21,400
either we'll have quite a few different filters that

795
00:35:21,400 --> 00:35:23,525
are happening in parallel, we'll get into it later,

796
00:35:23,525 --> 00:35:26,400
or we add a lot more layers in a dense way.

797
00:35:26,400 --> 00:35:28,135
But what we have found experimentally,

798
00:35:28,135 --> 00:35:29,760
and there's some nice theory around it,

799
00:35:29,760 --> 00:35:33,400
is that for things where this is [? an ?] [? appropriate ?]

800
00:35:33,400 --> 00:35:36,140
inductive bias, you can learn more efficiently.

801
00:35:36,140 --> 00:35:39,060
You get a better fit with less data.

802
00:35:39,060 --> 00:35:40,800
Cool.

803
00:35:40,800 --> 00:35:43,240
So what if we have color?

804
00:35:43,240 --> 00:35:45,820
I mean, we're really taking a very image perspective here.

805
00:35:45,820 --> 00:35:47,987
But essentially, what we're talking about with color

806
00:35:47,987 --> 00:35:51,180
is multiple input channels, so not just spatial extent,

807
00:35:51,180 --> 00:35:55,080
but you have a tensor that also has something like depth.

808
00:35:55,080 --> 00:35:58,780
So if you have multi-channel inputs-- so say you do have RGB.

809
00:35:58,780 --> 00:36:00,920
That's what we're showing here.

810
00:36:00,920 --> 00:36:04,040
What you do to do convolution over multi-channel inputs

811
00:36:04,040 --> 00:36:08,020
is you learn a different set of weights for each of those input

812
00:36:08,020 --> 00:36:08,840
channels.

813
00:36:08,840 --> 00:36:13,340
And then you sum over all of that to get the output value.

814
00:36:13,340 --> 00:36:16,380
So you sum essentially over the convolutions

815
00:36:16,380 --> 00:36:19,020
for each of the input channels to get the output value

816
00:36:19,020 --> 00:36:21,180
you're summing per channel.

817
00:36:21,180 --> 00:36:25,060
So now what that looks like is you

818
00:36:25,060 --> 00:36:28,920
would still have a single output value for each filter.

819
00:36:28,920 --> 00:36:33,300
But now the filter is going to have per channel weights that

820
00:36:33,300 --> 00:36:34,180
are learned.

821
00:36:34,180 --> 00:36:37,880
And you could arguably force them to be shared.

822
00:36:37,880 --> 00:36:41,180
But often, we let them be separate.

823
00:36:41,180 --> 00:36:43,980
What about multi-channel outputs?

824
00:36:43,980 --> 00:36:47,998
So if instead of having just a single output

825
00:36:47,998 --> 00:36:49,540
of your convolutional layer, you want

826
00:36:49,540 --> 00:36:51,957
to have multiple channels of outputs,

827
00:36:51,957 --> 00:36:53,540
here what you actually do is you learn

828
00:36:53,540 --> 00:36:58,620
what we call a filter bank or a set of different output

829
00:36:58,620 --> 00:36:59,560
convolutions.

830
00:36:59,560 --> 00:37:05,180
So here instead of just learning a single linear layer,

831
00:37:05,180 --> 00:37:08,000
you're going to learn two in parallel, and each of them

832
00:37:08,000 --> 00:37:11,320
get to build their own output dimension.

833
00:37:11,320 --> 00:37:12,720
And so now you have a filter bank

834
00:37:12,720 --> 00:37:14,920
of let's say C different filters.

835
00:37:14,920 --> 00:37:17,240
And you get a different output activation

836
00:37:17,240 --> 00:37:20,800
for each of those filters from the same input data.

837
00:37:20,800 --> 00:37:22,380
So then you can generalize this.

838
00:37:22,380 --> 00:37:24,680
And generally, in most of our convolutional layers

839
00:37:24,680 --> 00:37:30,140
in deep learning, we have multi-input and multi-output

840
00:37:30,140 --> 00:37:31,280
functions.

841
00:37:31,280 --> 00:37:36,040
And so here you'll have essentially your output

842
00:37:36,040 --> 00:37:38,560
is going to have some number of dimensions

843
00:37:38,560 --> 00:37:41,560
or some number of feature channels.

844
00:37:41,560 --> 00:37:45,000
The input will have some number of input, dimensions or feature

845
00:37:45,000 --> 00:37:45,940
channels.

846
00:37:45,940 --> 00:37:49,560
And then you're going to have some number of filters

847
00:37:49,560 --> 00:37:52,760
that you convolve with that input to get this output.

848
00:37:52,760 --> 00:37:54,600
And the number of features that you have,

849
00:37:54,600 --> 00:37:58,440
you're going to have the number of output dimensions, number

850
00:37:58,440 --> 00:38:01,280
of features or filters.

851
00:38:01,280 --> 00:38:03,860
Each of those filters is going to have some kernel size.

852
00:38:03,860 --> 00:38:07,210
That's K here, so some of spatial dimensions.

853
00:38:07,210 --> 00:38:09,410
And then for each of them, you're

854
00:38:09,410 --> 00:38:13,130
going to have enough channels to match your input data.

855
00:38:13,130 --> 00:38:15,090
So let me show a few more examples

856
00:38:15,090 --> 00:38:16,410
of what this looks like.

857
00:38:16,410 --> 00:38:18,850
So here's, for example, a bank of two filters

858
00:38:18,850 --> 00:38:21,970
where you have multiple inputs and multiple outputs.

859
00:38:21,970 --> 00:38:24,510
So here you have two input dimensions.

860
00:38:24,510 --> 00:38:28,970
You've now learned this linear sum for your height

861
00:38:28,970 --> 00:38:32,850
by width or this kernel size, which in this case

862
00:38:32,850 --> 00:38:35,210
looks like 3 by 3.

863
00:38:35,210 --> 00:38:37,542
So each of these sets of weights, F1 and F2,

864
00:38:37,542 --> 00:38:39,250
those are learned separately, but they're

865
00:38:39,250 --> 00:38:42,010
shared across the entire image.

866
00:38:42,010 --> 00:38:44,370
And so what those feature maps might actually

867
00:38:44,370 --> 00:38:50,730
look like, they get increasingly complex, I would say.

868
00:38:50,730 --> 00:38:54,210
So here, this is your input.

869
00:38:54,210 --> 00:38:59,170
This is using an AlexNet model, so a convolutional model.

870
00:38:59,170 --> 00:39:01,170
Now you take that input.

871
00:39:01,170 --> 00:39:03,310
After the first convolutional layer,

872
00:39:03,310 --> 00:39:06,070
this is the number of feature banks that are learned

873
00:39:06,070 --> 00:39:08,990
or output channels that are learned.

874
00:39:08,990 --> 00:39:14,710
This initial input is convolved with this many different

875
00:39:14,710 --> 00:39:15,627
convolutional filters.

876
00:39:15,627 --> 00:39:18,168
And so now we're showing all of those different filters, what

877
00:39:18,168 --> 00:39:20,310
actually activates when you send in this image.

878
00:39:20,310 --> 00:39:22,477
And so you can see, I don't know, maybe some of them

879
00:39:22,477 --> 00:39:24,610
are capturing things like certain types of edges,

880
00:39:24,610 --> 00:39:26,870
certain types of structures.

881
00:39:26,870 --> 00:39:29,170
And then after that second convolutional layer,

882
00:39:29,170 --> 00:39:32,790
these things start to get maybe a little bit more complex.

883
00:39:32,790 --> 00:39:35,150
They can often be quite difficult to interpret.

884
00:39:35,150 --> 00:39:38,310
But every layer can be thought of as a set of C feature maps

885
00:39:38,310 --> 00:39:39,470
or channels.

886
00:39:39,470 --> 00:39:42,270
And every feature map is essentially

887
00:39:42,270 --> 00:39:43,787
like structural representation.

888
00:39:43,787 --> 00:39:44,370
It's an image.

889
00:39:44,370 --> 00:39:47,190
It has height and width.

890
00:39:47,190 --> 00:39:47,830
Yeah?

891
00:39:47,830 --> 00:39:50,750
AUDIENCE: How do you ensure that each feature map is

892
00:39:50,750 --> 00:39:52,990
uniquely different so that you have

893
00:39:52,990 --> 00:39:54,732
a set of ones that can combine?

894
00:39:54,732 --> 00:39:56,190
SARA BEERY: So how do you make sure

895
00:39:56,190 --> 00:39:58,830
that all of the different feature maps are different?

896
00:39:58,830 --> 00:40:02,250
So that's not actually something that's innately built in,

897
00:40:02,250 --> 00:40:05,730
but one of the ways that people will do this

898
00:40:05,730 --> 00:40:09,930
is through different types of regularization in the model,

899
00:40:09,930 --> 00:40:11,610
so sometimes things like dropout that

900
00:40:11,610 --> 00:40:17,290
tries to make sure that you don't make the model too brittle

901
00:40:17,290 --> 00:40:20,130
or reliant on different things.

902
00:40:20,130 --> 00:40:24,370
But usually, the model will learn each of them

903
00:40:24,370 --> 00:40:27,610
uniquely because that gives it more capacity.

904
00:40:27,610 --> 00:40:30,210
There's nothing in here forcing it to keep them all the same.

905
00:40:30,210 --> 00:40:35,410
And so why would it not learn to maximize the types of variants

906
00:40:35,410 --> 00:40:38,050
that it could have.

907
00:40:38,050 --> 00:40:40,450
Depending on the data, and the architecture,

908
00:40:40,450 --> 00:40:43,170
and your optimization, you can have feature collapse

909
00:40:43,170 --> 00:40:45,998
sometimes where some stuff just gets ignored that you might not

910
00:40:45,998 --> 00:40:46,790
want to be ignored.

911
00:40:46,790 --> 00:40:48,998
And then you can try to build in different mechanisms

912
00:40:48,998 --> 00:40:50,810
to make that not happen.

913
00:40:50,810 --> 00:40:54,810
All right, so we're going to do like a simple little quiz.

914
00:40:54,810 --> 00:41:00,050
So here's a layer.

915
00:41:00,050 --> 00:41:01,790
We have this input.

916
00:41:01,790 --> 00:41:05,650
It's three channels, so RGB by 128 by 128.

917
00:41:05,650 --> 00:41:08,390
Very standard image size for an input.

918
00:41:08,390 --> 00:41:11,330
And then we have a filter bank full of 3 by 3 filters.

919
00:41:11,330 --> 00:41:13,785
So the spatial extent of those filters is 3 by 3.

920
00:41:13,785 --> 00:41:15,410
This is often the way we talk about it.

921
00:41:15,410 --> 00:41:16,850
So we're not talking about the channel dimensions.

922
00:41:16,850 --> 00:41:18,870
We just talk about the spatial extent

923
00:41:18,870 --> 00:41:20,670
of the size of the filters.

924
00:41:20,670 --> 00:41:25,710
And then our output is 96 channels by 128

925
00:41:25,710 --> 00:41:28,150
by 128, so by height and width.

926
00:41:28,150 --> 00:41:36,470
So, first, how many parameters does each of those filters have/

927
00:41:36,470 --> 00:41:37,327
AUDIENCE: 9.

928
00:41:37,327 --> 00:41:37,910
SARA BEERY: 9.

929
00:41:37,910 --> 00:41:39,290
So why 9?

930
00:41:39,290 --> 00:41:40,750
AUDIENCE: It's 3 by 3.

931
00:41:40,750 --> 00:41:42,917
SARA BEERY: But how many input channels did we have?

932
00:41:42,917 --> 00:41:45,430
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

933
00:41:45,430 --> 00:41:46,910
SARA BEERY: All good.

934
00:41:46,910 --> 00:41:48,910
Yeah.

935
00:41:48,910 --> 00:41:52,810
Every single one of these filters has 27 parameters.

936
00:41:52,810 --> 00:41:54,970
We put this in because it's a little confusing.

937
00:41:54,970 --> 00:41:57,150
We talk about the filters being 3 by 3.

938
00:41:57,150 --> 00:41:58,930
But the thing is when--

939
00:41:58,930 --> 00:42:00,230
this is just convention.

940
00:42:00,230 --> 00:42:02,290
When we talk about filter size, we often only

941
00:42:02,290 --> 00:42:04,305
talk about the spatial extent of the filters.

942
00:42:04,305 --> 00:42:05,930
And the assumption is we don't actually

943
00:42:05,930 --> 00:42:10,077
convolve in channel space, though, of course, you could.

944
00:42:10,077 --> 00:42:11,910
That's something you could totally build in.

945
00:42:11,910 --> 00:42:14,202
So maybe you have something like a hyperspectral input.

946
00:42:14,202 --> 00:42:16,890
You actually want convolution across the spectral bands.

947
00:42:16,890 --> 00:42:18,650
You could build that into your model.

948
00:42:18,650 --> 00:42:26,170
But standard, we often use independent per input channel

949
00:42:26,170 --> 00:42:27,530
weights that are learned.

950
00:42:27,530 --> 00:42:30,970
And so then we only talk about the spatial extent.

951
00:42:30,970 --> 00:42:33,710
So the filter banks are 3 by 3.

952
00:42:33,710 --> 00:42:39,630
Now how many filters are in our filter bank?

953
00:42:39,630 --> 00:42:40,508
Yeah?

954
00:42:40,508 --> 00:42:41,050
AUDIENCE: 96.

955
00:42:41,050 --> 00:42:43,450
SARA BEERY: 96, exactly.

956
00:42:43,450 --> 00:42:48,170
There are 96 outputs here, which means we get 96 filters.

957
00:42:48,170 --> 00:42:51,490
And then each of those filters will have those 27 parameters.

958
00:42:51,490 --> 00:42:55,210
So you're doing this-- for each of those 27 parameter input

959
00:42:55,210 --> 00:42:57,790
filters, you're running those over the entire image,

960
00:42:57,790 --> 00:43:00,902
and you are now taking a linear sum over all three

961
00:43:00,902 --> 00:43:03,110
of those channels, and you're getting a single output

962
00:43:03,110 --> 00:43:03,770
dimension.

963
00:43:03,770 --> 00:43:06,050
And so if you have 96 output channels,

964
00:43:06,050 --> 00:43:08,950
you need 96 different filters.

965
00:43:08,950 --> 00:43:10,640
Nice.

966
00:43:10,640 --> 00:43:11,490
Cool.

967
00:43:11,490 --> 00:43:19,530
So high level, filter sizes, if you're mapping from some x of l,

968
00:43:19,530 --> 00:43:23,990
so this current layer where you have C sub l channels

969
00:43:23,990 --> 00:43:27,350
and then N by M output dimensions

970
00:43:27,350 --> 00:43:31,550
and you're going to map to the next layer where you have some C

971
00:43:31,550 --> 00:43:34,230
sub L plus 1 output dimensions and N

972
00:43:34,230 --> 00:43:37,190
by M spatial dimensions and your filter

973
00:43:37,190 --> 00:43:41,310
has spatial extent K1 by K2, then the number

974
00:43:41,310 --> 00:43:46,750
of parameters per filter will be K1 times K2 times C sub l.

975
00:43:46,750 --> 00:43:49,310
And then the number of filters will just be C sub l

976
00:43:49,310 --> 00:43:52,550
plus 1, the number of output channels that you have.

977
00:43:52,550 --> 00:43:53,190
Yeah?

978
00:43:53,190 --> 00:43:58,730
AUDIENCE: So I [INAUDIBLE] square

979
00:43:58,730 --> 00:44:00,990
filters when dealing with convolutions.

980
00:44:00,990 --> 00:44:03,930
I'm just curious where you have rectangular filters

981
00:44:03,930 --> 00:44:05,530
or [INAUDIBLE]?

982
00:44:05,530 --> 00:44:07,970
SARA BEERY: Absolutely, normally, the filters

983
00:44:07,970 --> 00:44:08,690
are square.

984
00:44:08,690 --> 00:44:10,390
That is totally standard.

985
00:44:10,390 --> 00:44:11,930
They don't have to be.

986
00:44:11,930 --> 00:44:15,530
A convolution can be defined in a way that is not square.

987
00:44:15,530 --> 00:44:18,910
But I would say normally we see square convolutions.

988
00:44:18,910 --> 00:44:20,470
Yeah.

989
00:44:20,470 --> 00:44:22,290
Cool.

990
00:44:22,290 --> 00:44:26,970
So you have say your input image.

991
00:44:26,970 --> 00:44:30,010
Now you're going to run it through a first layer.

992
00:44:30,010 --> 00:44:32,350
So essentially, for each of your filters,

993
00:44:32,350 --> 00:44:36,950
you have a red, a green, and a blue filter.

994
00:44:36,950 --> 00:44:38,150
Those get applied.

995
00:44:38,150 --> 00:44:40,390
And then you can think about, OK,

996
00:44:40,390 --> 00:44:43,645
so now if you take just one of those filters, that's

997
00:44:43,645 --> 00:44:45,770
going to give you just a single one of those output

998
00:44:45,770 --> 00:44:49,650
maps in your feature maps at layer 1.

999
00:44:49,650 --> 00:44:51,050
A single one of those output maps

1000
00:44:51,050 --> 00:44:54,870
is going to correspond to one of those filters over RGB.

1001
00:44:54,870 --> 00:44:57,690
And now if you run that a second layer,

1002
00:44:57,690 --> 00:45:00,150
second convolutional layer, now you

1003
00:45:00,150 --> 00:45:03,050
have layer 2, your whole sets of features.

1004
00:45:03,050 --> 00:45:05,290
Now each of those are going to be-- instead of RGB,

1005
00:45:05,290 --> 00:45:09,110
they're going to be across the entire set of feature maps

1006
00:45:09,110 --> 00:45:10,590
in your first layer.

1007
00:45:10,590 --> 00:45:13,910
But then one of the outputs after that will only

1008
00:45:13,910 --> 00:45:17,190
correspond to a single one of those C2 filters.

1009
00:45:17,190 --> 00:45:19,670
So you can see how the actual representation gets

1010
00:45:19,670 --> 00:45:23,110
highly complex, highly quickly.

1011
00:45:23,110 --> 00:45:25,130
Even just one or two layers of depth,

1012
00:45:25,130 --> 00:45:27,270
when you have convolution, it can

1013
00:45:27,270 --> 00:45:30,050
map really complex functions.

1014
00:45:32,710 --> 00:45:34,990
So another thing that we often use

1015
00:45:34,990 --> 00:45:37,310
when we're building convolutional neural networks

1016
00:45:37,310 --> 00:45:39,910
is this idea of pooling.

1017
00:45:39,910 --> 00:45:44,650
So here, what we have is we have this convolutional layer.

1018
00:45:44,650 --> 00:45:47,830
So you have your convolutional weights, you have your bias,

1019
00:45:47,830 --> 00:45:50,790
and then you have your point-wise non-linearity.

1020
00:45:50,790 --> 00:45:53,290
But then one of the things that you can do

1021
00:45:53,290 --> 00:45:55,650
is you can do what's called pooling over those output

1022
00:45:55,650 --> 00:45:56,310
features.

1023
00:45:56,310 --> 00:45:57,730
One of the reasons you might want to do that

1024
00:45:57,730 --> 00:45:59,813
is because you can see that the dimensionality can

1025
00:45:59,813 --> 00:46:02,210
get really large, really fast.

1026
00:46:02,210 --> 00:46:04,730
And so one thing, very, very common thing,

1027
00:46:04,730 --> 00:46:07,410
is something we call max pooling, where here you

1028
00:46:07,410 --> 00:46:12,850
just take some local window and you just use the maximum value.

1029
00:46:12,850 --> 00:46:15,610
And you use that to represent your output.

1030
00:46:15,610 --> 00:46:18,730
Or you can also think about something like mean pooling.

1031
00:46:18,730 --> 00:46:20,930
And here you're just taking the average

1032
00:46:20,930 --> 00:46:23,450
over some smaller local window.

1033
00:46:23,450 --> 00:46:24,830
So why do you want to do pooling?

1034
00:46:24,830 --> 00:46:26,497
So one thing that's really nice about it

1035
00:46:26,497 --> 00:46:28,690
is pooling across spatial locations

1036
00:46:28,690 --> 00:46:31,130
gives you some stability.

1037
00:46:31,130 --> 00:46:39,050
So for example, here, if you're taking the max over this window,

1038
00:46:39,050 --> 00:46:42,150
you're still going to identify that there's an edge there.

1039
00:46:42,150 --> 00:46:47,250
There's still this white value that will get passed through,

1040
00:46:47,250 --> 00:46:51,880
and that will be stable as the inputs move.

1041
00:46:51,880 --> 00:46:55,100
So you'll get a large response to something

1042
00:46:55,100 --> 00:46:56,560
like an edge filter.

1043
00:46:56,560 --> 00:46:59,220
Regardless of the exact position of that edge,

1044
00:46:59,220 --> 00:47:01,520
you get some more stability in the outputs.

1045
00:47:07,220 --> 00:47:10,940
And then sometimes we also will do pooling across channels.

1046
00:47:10,940 --> 00:47:12,860
And one cool thing you can think about here

1047
00:47:12,860 --> 00:47:14,540
is if you pool across feature channels

1048
00:47:14,540 --> 00:47:17,720
where all the feature channels are looking for those edges

1049
00:47:17,720 --> 00:47:20,260
but across different orientations of that edge

1050
00:47:20,260 --> 00:47:24,800
filter, then you can get some new kinds of invariances.

1051
00:47:24,800 --> 00:47:27,420
So for example, this would give you

1052
00:47:27,420 --> 00:47:30,740
a filter that was finding edges and was

1053
00:47:30,740 --> 00:47:33,620
invariant to the orientation of the edge.

1054
00:47:33,620 --> 00:47:36,540
Seems nice.

1055
00:47:36,540 --> 00:47:37,460
Cool.

1056
00:47:37,460 --> 00:47:42,240
So now if we actually look at a lot of the time

1057
00:47:42,240 --> 00:47:44,380
what computation and neural network looks like,

1058
00:47:44,380 --> 00:47:48,180
a convolutional neural network, we will usually take an input,

1059
00:47:48,180 --> 00:47:51,660
and then we will run it through stacks of this building block.

1060
00:47:51,660 --> 00:47:55,080
And this building block is some computation

1061
00:47:55,080 --> 00:47:59,920
over some set of filters, a nonlinear activation, so a ReLU,

1062
00:47:59,920 --> 00:48:01,640
and then something like pooling.

1063
00:48:01,640 --> 00:48:05,200
And then we'll repeat that over and over and over again.

1064
00:48:05,200 --> 00:48:08,457
But this is a really large network

1065
00:48:08,457 --> 00:48:09,540
that has a lot of density.

1066
00:48:09,540 --> 00:48:12,620
And as we maybe add more feature channels as we keep going,

1067
00:48:12,620 --> 00:48:13,998
this could get really big.

1068
00:48:13,998 --> 00:48:16,040
So particularly, if you're trying to do something

1069
00:48:16,040 --> 00:48:19,400
like classification, where the only thing you want to get out

1070
00:48:19,400 --> 00:48:22,420
is a low rank representation of the input,

1071
00:48:22,420 --> 00:48:27,360
just something like a word or a specific one hot encoding, here

1072
00:48:27,360 --> 00:48:30,300
we actually often use something called downsampling,

1073
00:48:30,300 --> 00:48:32,320
where we assume that we don't actually

1074
00:48:32,320 --> 00:48:37,320
need that high-dimensional input structure at every dimension

1075
00:48:37,320 --> 00:48:38,300
of the network.

1076
00:48:38,300 --> 00:48:40,500
And so here, instead of just pooling,

1077
00:48:40,500 --> 00:48:42,640
we might also downsample.

1078
00:48:42,640 --> 00:48:44,240
And what that looks like--

1079
00:48:44,240 --> 00:48:47,440
so here if you have pooling, you've now done something

1080
00:48:47,440 --> 00:48:51,720
like your convolutional layer, your activation function,

1081
00:48:51,720 --> 00:48:53,860
and then something like max pooling,

1082
00:48:53,860 --> 00:48:56,680
then downsampling would look something like this.

1083
00:48:56,680 --> 00:48:59,940
So maybe you would only keep some subset

1084
00:48:59,940 --> 00:49:03,140
of those actual values.

1085
00:49:03,140 --> 00:49:05,700
And then instead of doing the pooling across all of them,

1086
00:49:05,700 --> 00:49:10,180
you can also just do just straight downsampling.

1087
00:49:10,180 --> 00:49:12,020
And one way that you can actually

1088
00:49:12,020 --> 00:49:16,020
do both pooling and downsampling together

1089
00:49:16,020 --> 00:49:19,100
is through something called a strided operation.

1090
00:49:19,100 --> 00:49:21,940
So this actually combines something like convolution

1091
00:49:21,940 --> 00:49:23,720
or pooling and downsampling.

1092
00:49:23,720 --> 00:49:26,900
And the way that it does that is instead of going and sliding

1093
00:49:26,900 --> 00:49:31,420
your filter by a single pixel every single time, instead,

1094
00:49:31,420 --> 00:49:34,820
you increase what we call the stride of the filter.

1095
00:49:34,820 --> 00:49:38,940
So now you operate your convolution.

1096
00:49:38,940 --> 00:49:41,500
And then instead of just moving one, you might move two

1097
00:49:41,500 --> 00:49:43,317
or you might move five.

1098
00:49:43,317 --> 00:49:44,900
In this particular example, here we're

1099
00:49:44,900 --> 00:49:46,880
showing a stride that's quite big.

1100
00:49:46,880 --> 00:49:49,760
Usually, for these models, we often

1101
00:49:49,760 --> 00:49:52,120
have that stride be somewhat overlapping,

1102
00:49:52,120 --> 00:49:54,240
so you don't want to lose any of the context

1103
00:49:54,240 --> 00:49:55,323
from the original input.

1104
00:49:55,323 --> 00:49:57,740
So we'll have that stride be something that's overlapping.

1105
00:49:57,740 --> 00:50:01,420
But maybe your input kernel is like 7 by 7.

1106
00:50:01,420 --> 00:50:05,040
Your stride might be 5, something like that.

1107
00:50:05,040 --> 00:50:11,960
And so here you're combining that pooling, that convolution,

1108
00:50:11,960 --> 00:50:15,400
and the downsampling into a single element.

1109
00:50:15,400 --> 00:50:17,880
And you can also think about doing something that we

1110
00:50:17,880 --> 00:50:20,600
call using a dilated filter.

1111
00:50:20,600 --> 00:50:22,520
And essentially, all this is it's just

1112
00:50:22,520 --> 00:50:25,880
a filter where we've set some of the values to 0 and baked that

1113
00:50:25,880 --> 00:50:27,240
into our model.

1114
00:50:27,240 --> 00:50:30,515
And this gives you a larger receptive field, so what

1115
00:50:30,515 --> 00:50:31,640
I was talking about before.

1116
00:50:31,640 --> 00:50:34,120
You get more information from a larger spatial context

1117
00:50:34,120 --> 00:50:36,620
or spatial extent with fewer parameters.

1118
00:50:36,620 --> 00:50:37,860
There's less flexibility.

1119
00:50:37,860 --> 00:50:39,580
There's less parameters that are learned,

1120
00:50:39,580 --> 00:50:44,080
but you're kind of getting that larger context window.

1121
00:50:44,080 --> 00:50:48,940
And that's kind of a trade-off that you can optimize for.

1122
00:50:48,940 --> 00:50:50,220
Yeah?

1123
00:50:50,220 --> 00:50:51,380
AUDIENCE: Oh, sorry.

1124
00:50:51,380 --> 00:50:55,820
So when you downsample, you cannot go back to the previous

1125
00:50:55,820 --> 00:50:59,160
dimension to input dimension unless you give information

1126
00:50:59,160 --> 00:51:01,597
across the network, right?

1127
00:51:01,597 --> 00:51:03,680
SARA BEERY: We'll talk about that in a little bit.

1128
00:51:03,680 --> 00:51:05,940
So the question was if you downsample,

1129
00:51:05,940 --> 00:51:08,220
you can't get back to the original input dimension

1130
00:51:08,220 --> 00:51:10,880
unless you add some more information.

1131
00:51:10,880 --> 00:51:12,940
That's not necessarily true.

1132
00:51:12,940 --> 00:51:14,852
But you might not do it very well.

1133
00:51:14,852 --> 00:51:16,560
So we'll talk about that in a little bit.

1134
00:51:16,560 --> 00:51:18,140
AUDIENCE: What are the current norms

1135
00:51:18,140 --> 00:51:25,685
for when it's acceptable to use striding [INAUDIBLE]?

1136
00:51:25,685 --> 00:51:28,060
SARA BEERY: What are the current norms for when you would

1137
00:51:28,060 --> 00:51:29,980
or would not use a stride?

1138
00:51:29,980 --> 00:51:34,180
So I would say that this search space of possible architectures

1139
00:51:34,180 --> 00:51:35,540
is vast.

1140
00:51:35,540 --> 00:51:38,180
And even if we only consider the building blocks

1141
00:51:38,180 --> 00:51:40,980
we've talked about so far, which is like linear layers

1142
00:51:40,980 --> 00:51:42,980
and convolutional layers--

1143
00:51:42,980 --> 00:51:45,100
and then you can have varied input sizes,

1144
00:51:45,100 --> 00:51:46,860
varied depth, various numbers of channels.

1145
00:51:46,860 --> 00:51:49,152
All of a sudden, you already have a really large search

1146
00:51:49,152 --> 00:51:50,240
space.

1147
00:51:50,240 --> 00:51:51,800
There's entire fields of research

1148
00:51:51,800 --> 00:51:53,800
called neural architecture search that actually

1149
00:51:53,800 --> 00:51:58,440
specifically tried to optimize over the space of architectures

1150
00:51:58,440 --> 00:52:00,220
to find the best possible solution.

1151
00:52:00,220 --> 00:52:03,720
Pretty computationally expensive to actually train those.

1152
00:52:03,720 --> 00:52:08,840
But I would say that one of the things that's kind of proven out

1153
00:52:08,840 --> 00:52:13,220
in the literature over the last of maybe 10 years

1154
00:52:13,220 --> 00:52:15,000
just experimentally is there seem

1155
00:52:15,000 --> 00:52:20,440
to be some standards that seem to be trends that are long held.

1156
00:52:20,440 --> 00:52:22,940
So for example, in the original AlexNet paper,

1157
00:52:22,940 --> 00:52:24,400
the filters were quite big.

1158
00:52:24,400 --> 00:52:26,080
They had pretty large spatial extent,

1159
00:52:26,080 --> 00:52:28,320
and then there were less depth in the layers.

1160
00:52:28,320 --> 00:52:29,800
One of the things that we've seen more and more

1161
00:52:29,800 --> 00:52:32,160
is now we actually often see filters that are reasonably

1162
00:52:32,160 --> 00:52:37,080
small, 7 or 5 spatial extent.

1163
00:52:37,080 --> 00:52:40,620
But maybe then they're actually stacked quite deep.

1164
00:52:40,620 --> 00:52:43,400
So then you end up with really large receptive fields,

1165
00:52:43,400 --> 00:52:48,140
even though the initial filters were spatially quite small.

1166
00:52:48,140 --> 00:52:49,740
And then with striding, again, it's

1167
00:52:49,740 --> 00:52:52,420
just that trade off between the amount of computation

1168
00:52:52,420 --> 00:52:53,840
and the receptive field.

1169
00:52:53,840 --> 00:52:57,182
So there's different kind of optimality.

1170
00:52:57,182 --> 00:52:59,140
One of the things you can do is you can go look

1171
00:52:59,140 --> 00:53:01,915
at a bunch of the standard architectures,

1172
00:53:01,915 --> 00:53:04,040
and often they'll have these architecture diagrams.

1173
00:53:04,040 --> 00:53:07,980
You've seen them before probably where it's like the layer,

1174
00:53:07,980 --> 00:53:10,620
and then it'll have just like a layer size for each thing.

1175
00:53:10,620 --> 00:53:13,420
And you can look at, what are the similarities

1176
00:53:13,420 --> 00:53:15,640
across the structures of these different models?

1177
00:53:15,640 --> 00:53:17,560
AUDIENCE: Does the complexity of the picture matter at all,

1178
00:53:17,560 --> 00:53:19,060
for example, the first example, you

1179
00:53:19,060 --> 00:53:22,620
gave, which is Times Square versus the nature?

1180
00:53:22,620 --> 00:53:24,420
SARA BEERY: Yeah, experimentally, yes.

1181
00:53:24,420 --> 00:53:29,580
So if you have really non-complex pictures like CIFAR,

1182
00:53:29,580 --> 00:53:33,860
for example, these 100 by 100 really simple photos,

1183
00:53:33,860 --> 00:53:38,780
then the network itself often needs to be much less complex.

1184
00:53:38,780 --> 00:53:41,080
If fine-grained feature details actually

1185
00:53:41,080 --> 00:53:42,800
matter a lot, which happens for us a lot

1186
00:53:42,800 --> 00:53:45,060
with the species images, for example,

1187
00:53:45,060 --> 00:53:46,560
then one of the things that actually

1188
00:53:46,560 --> 00:53:48,840
tends to matter more than almost anything else

1189
00:53:48,840 --> 00:53:51,160
is the input image size that you use.

1190
00:53:51,160 --> 00:53:54,020
So a lot of these models, the input image size,

1191
00:53:54,020 --> 00:53:55,960
if you scale it up, then the computation

1192
00:53:55,960 --> 00:53:59,720
scales up quite a lot with that.

1193
00:53:59,720 --> 00:54:02,607
So we'll often downsample images to some smaller size

1194
00:54:02,607 --> 00:54:04,440
before we run them through a network as part

1195
00:54:04,440 --> 00:54:05,720
of the pre-processing.

1196
00:54:05,720 --> 00:54:08,877
But we find that increasing the scale of that input image

1197
00:54:08,877 --> 00:54:10,960
and then maybe downscaling some of the computation

1198
00:54:10,960 --> 00:54:12,260
is sometimes a better trade-off.

1199
00:54:12,260 --> 00:54:14,218
If you really care about fine-grained features,

1200
00:54:14,218 --> 00:54:16,920
you don't want to lose that detail.

1201
00:54:16,920 --> 00:54:18,040
Yeah?

1202
00:54:18,040 --> 00:54:20,480
AUDIENCE: So normally, in signal processing,

1203
00:54:20,480 --> 00:54:24,540
we're [INAUDIBLE] filters for a given purpose.

1204
00:54:24,540 --> 00:54:25,510
[INAUDIBLE]

1205
00:54:28,800 --> 00:54:35,040
And in this case, the weights, that would be [INAUDIBLE].

1206
00:54:35,040 --> 00:54:39,790
You mentioned earlier the model will learn [INAUDIBLE].

1207
00:54:39,790 --> 00:54:41,850
So how would you--

1208
00:54:41,850 --> 00:54:45,210
or do we include inductive biases

1209
00:54:45,210 --> 00:54:47,210
in how we train our models so that we

1210
00:54:47,210 --> 00:54:50,430
can design our models to then do a certain purpose,

1211
00:54:50,430 --> 00:54:56,290
no different than designing an [INAUDIBLE] for a signal?

1212
00:54:56,290 --> 00:54:57,723
How does the model [INAUDIBLE]?

1213
00:54:57,723 --> 00:54:59,390
SARA BEERY: Yeah, it's a great question.

1214
00:54:59,390 --> 00:55:02,030
So the question was in signal processing,

1215
00:55:02,030 --> 00:55:06,030
we're often very carefully handcrafting our filters

1216
00:55:06,030 --> 00:55:08,690
so that they are capturing a certain dimension of the signal

1217
00:55:08,690 --> 00:55:11,370
that we want to capture, whereas here we're

1218
00:55:11,370 --> 00:55:13,970
talking about filters that are actually being learned.

1219
00:55:13,970 --> 00:55:15,510
And what's the trade-off?

1220
00:55:15,510 --> 00:55:18,570
When does it make sense to try to actually craft

1221
00:55:18,570 --> 00:55:22,490
the filters to match our intuition about the problem?

1222
00:55:22,490 --> 00:55:25,490
So what you're talking about is actually this massive paradigm

1223
00:55:25,490 --> 00:55:29,810
shift that happened maybe 10, 15 years ago in the field where

1224
00:55:29,810 --> 00:55:34,610
we went from most of the work was

1225
00:55:34,610 --> 00:55:38,248
on what we called feature engineering in computer vision

1226
00:55:38,248 --> 00:55:40,790
or in deep learning, where you would be building these really

1227
00:55:40,790 --> 00:55:44,750
handcrafted structures that then you would combine together

1228
00:55:44,750 --> 00:55:48,390
into some larger system that would recognize stuff.

1229
00:55:48,390 --> 00:55:51,490
And when we started to see that filters,

1230
00:55:51,490 --> 00:55:54,370
just like these models being learned end to end

1231
00:55:54,370 --> 00:55:58,630
were more predictive, it was a bit of like a catastrophe

1232
00:55:58,630 --> 00:56:00,210
emotionally for many researchers.

1233
00:56:00,210 --> 00:56:02,030
Because you were like, no, like I

1234
00:56:02,030 --> 00:56:04,470
spent my life learning how to craft these filters

1235
00:56:04,470 --> 00:56:06,570
in this really specific way.

1236
00:56:06,570 --> 00:56:08,670
I don't want to just cram a bunch of data

1237
00:56:08,670 --> 00:56:10,805
through a structure and have that be learned

1238
00:56:10,805 --> 00:56:11,930
and then have it be better.

1239
00:56:11,930 --> 00:56:16,950
And it turns out that actually the trade-off often comes

1240
00:56:16,950 --> 00:56:18,630
down to how much data you have.

1241
00:56:18,630 --> 00:56:21,450
So if you don't have enough data to learn the filters,

1242
00:56:21,450 --> 00:56:26,430
well, then more handcrafting, more knowledge, more inductive

1243
00:56:26,430 --> 00:56:30,270
bias tends to be better because you actually

1244
00:56:30,270 --> 00:56:31,890
can put more constraints on the system

1245
00:56:31,890 --> 00:56:33,950
so that you can optimize better with less data.

1246
00:56:33,950 --> 00:56:37,450
But if you're in a space where you can get huge amounts of data

1247
00:56:37,450 --> 00:56:40,690
to learn from, often, we don't know as much

1248
00:56:40,690 --> 00:56:43,530
as we think we know about what optimality might be in terms

1249
00:56:43,530 --> 00:56:45,530
of the filters that are learned, especially when

1250
00:56:45,530 --> 00:56:47,738
you think about-- it can be really intuitive to think

1251
00:56:47,738 --> 00:56:49,370
about crafting a single filter.

1252
00:56:49,370 --> 00:56:54,530
But how would you handcraft like the seventh convolutional layer

1253
00:56:54,530 --> 00:56:58,590
in a 128 layer network for a single channel?

1254
00:56:58,590 --> 00:57:02,378
It very rapidly gets to a place where the system is so complex

1255
00:57:02,378 --> 00:57:04,170
that it's very difficult to build intuition

1256
00:57:04,170 --> 00:57:06,950
about what it should be for us.

1257
00:57:06,950 --> 00:57:07,450
Cool.

1258
00:57:10,970 --> 00:57:12,110
So receptive fields.

1259
00:57:12,110 --> 00:57:14,170
So one of the things we talked about

1260
00:57:14,170 --> 00:57:18,410
is if you let of input size of the patch get too small,

1261
00:57:18,410 --> 00:57:20,290
you maybe won't be able to actually identify

1262
00:57:20,290 --> 00:57:21,890
the things you care about.

1263
00:57:21,890 --> 00:57:24,970
So in this particular instance, say maybe the input

1264
00:57:24,970 --> 00:57:26,890
patch is this entire bird.

1265
00:57:26,890 --> 00:57:30,930
And then we're running it through convolutional network,

1266
00:57:30,930 --> 00:57:32,590
ReLU, convolutional layer.

1267
00:57:32,590 --> 00:57:34,830
And then we get some output.

1268
00:57:34,830 --> 00:57:37,090
And that output for that patch is bird.

1269
00:57:37,090 --> 00:57:41,230
So here what we would say is the receptive field

1270
00:57:41,230 --> 00:57:46,532
of that x2 output is the input patch there.

1271
00:57:46,532 --> 00:57:47,990
Because receptive field essentially

1272
00:57:47,990 --> 00:57:50,870
just says what parts of the initial input

1273
00:57:50,870 --> 00:57:54,050
have any influence on this part of the output.

1274
00:57:54,050 --> 00:57:55,470
Because we're doing this in a way

1275
00:57:55,470 --> 00:57:57,170
that's translation invariant.

1276
00:57:57,170 --> 00:58:00,510
We're doing the same computation across different patches.

1277
00:58:00,510 --> 00:58:03,590
Outside of that receptive field, this model

1278
00:58:03,590 --> 00:58:05,610
doesn't have any input information.

1279
00:58:05,610 --> 00:58:08,110
So if there was something just outside of that field of view

1280
00:58:08,110 --> 00:58:11,255
that was really informative and it wasn't actually in here,

1281
00:58:11,255 --> 00:58:12,630
then there's no way for the model

1282
00:58:12,630 --> 00:58:15,550
to get information outside of that receptive field.

1283
00:58:15,550 --> 00:58:19,370
You can also talk about the receptive field of input layers.

1284
00:58:19,370 --> 00:58:22,210
So you can say, OK, like in this first layer,

1285
00:58:22,210 --> 00:58:24,510
the receptive field of that component

1286
00:58:24,510 --> 00:58:28,550
of the output of that first layer is that part of the input.

1287
00:58:28,550 --> 00:58:34,450
And what that looks like-- as you pass through the network,

1288
00:58:34,450 --> 00:58:36,870
you can think about looking at the activations,

1289
00:58:36,870 --> 00:58:40,370
and you can see that they go from being very fine grained

1290
00:58:40,370 --> 00:58:42,110
or very detailed in the early layers

1291
00:58:42,110 --> 00:58:46,130
because maybe you're running them through convolutions

1292
00:58:46,130 --> 00:58:47,550
that are much more local.

1293
00:58:47,550 --> 00:58:51,710
And then as you get further out or further along in AlexNet,

1294
00:58:51,710 --> 00:58:54,410
VGG 16, ResNet 18, what you start to see

1295
00:58:54,410 --> 00:58:59,010
are feature activations that are kind of much more diffuse.

1296
00:58:59,010 --> 00:59:00,930
And this has to do with that receptive field

1297
00:59:00,930 --> 00:59:03,690
because now each one of these output dimensions

1298
00:59:03,690 --> 00:59:06,530
is actually collecting really complex information

1299
00:59:06,530 --> 00:59:08,970
and mapping it through a bunch of different layers

1300
00:59:08,970 --> 00:59:10,490
from this input.

1301
00:59:10,490 --> 00:59:13,810
So maybe you're getting output feature activations

1302
00:59:13,810 --> 00:59:18,130
that are more semantically meaningful but less

1303
00:59:18,130 --> 00:59:24,370
affected by subtle texture or variation in the initial input

1304
00:59:24,370 --> 00:59:26,130
image possibly.

1305
00:59:26,130 --> 00:59:27,430
Yeah?

1306
00:59:27,430 --> 00:59:31,967
AUDIENCE: Similar computation to how you do Gaussian blurring?

1307
00:59:31,967 --> 00:59:33,550
SARA BEERY: So the question is if it's

1308
00:59:33,550 --> 00:59:35,842
similar in computation to how you do Gaussian blurring.

1309
00:59:38,550 --> 00:59:41,953
I think at a high level, like the intuition

1310
00:59:41,953 --> 00:59:43,870
is a little similar, but computationally, it's

1311
00:59:43,870 --> 00:59:45,070
quite different.

1312
00:59:45,070 --> 00:59:45,790
Yeah?

1313
00:59:45,790 --> 00:59:49,710
AUDIENCE: In that last image, are those, 1, 6, and 8,

1314
00:59:49,710 --> 00:59:55,830
are-- the ones on the right, are those the same pixel size

1315
00:59:55,830 --> 00:59:59,710
as the initial inputs, or are they downsampled and you just

1316
00:59:59,710 --> 01:00:01,210
resize them to all be the same?

1317
01:00:01,210 --> 01:00:02,630
SARA BEERY: We've resized them all to be the same size.

1318
01:00:02,630 --> 01:00:04,880
But it actually is very dependent on the architecture.

1319
01:00:04,880 --> 01:00:09,110
So ResNets, the actual spatial extent, once you get to block 8

1320
01:00:09,110 --> 01:00:10,090
is much lower.

1321
01:00:10,090 --> 01:00:13,990
And then there's a lot more channels versus the AlexNet one.

1322
01:00:13,990 --> 01:00:14,850
Yeah.

1323
01:00:14,850 --> 01:00:15,575
Cool.

1324
01:00:15,575 --> 01:00:16,950
So in the last little bit, I want

1325
01:00:16,950 --> 01:00:19,070
to talk through at a pretty high level

1326
01:00:19,070 --> 01:00:21,830
a bunch of these different high-level ideas, maybe some

1327
01:00:21,830 --> 01:00:25,110
of these what one of you mentioned, some of these things

1328
01:00:25,110 --> 01:00:32,210
that have tended to continue to be used over time.

1329
01:00:32,210 --> 01:00:35,570
So we talked about one very, very classic version

1330
01:00:35,570 --> 01:00:37,170
of a convolutional neural network

1331
01:00:37,170 --> 01:00:40,610
is something like a classification network.

1332
01:00:40,610 --> 01:00:42,930
And most classification networks have

1333
01:00:42,930 --> 01:00:44,510
something like this structure.

1334
01:00:44,510 --> 01:00:48,410
We'll have filters, some sort of point-wise non-linearity,

1335
01:00:48,410 --> 01:00:50,230
and then some form of downsampling.

1336
01:00:50,230 --> 01:00:52,147
And we run through a bunch of different layers

1337
01:00:52,147 --> 01:00:55,130
like that to get out some of output classification.

1338
01:00:55,130 --> 01:00:59,210
But another really common structure in using convolutions

1339
01:00:59,210 --> 01:01:03,610
is what we call an encoder-decoder architecture.

1340
01:01:03,610 --> 01:01:06,410
So here what you're doing is you're taking that input

1341
01:01:06,410 --> 01:01:09,970
and you're doing convolution, non-linearity, subsampling

1342
01:01:09,970 --> 01:01:12,850
or downsampling to get down to some very

1343
01:01:12,850 --> 01:01:16,690
low dimensional representation, for example this z space vector.

1344
01:01:16,690 --> 01:01:19,350
And then in the decoder component,

1345
01:01:19,350 --> 01:01:22,070
you're going to take that low dimensional representation,

1346
01:01:22,070 --> 01:01:25,570
and you're going to now do convolution non-linearity

1347
01:01:25,570 --> 01:01:27,170
upsampling.

1348
01:01:27,170 --> 01:01:29,030
So in that upsampling component, you're

1349
01:01:29,030 --> 01:01:31,790
actually just increasing the spatial extent of each

1350
01:01:31,790 --> 01:01:33,190
of the layers going forward.

1351
01:01:33,190 --> 01:01:37,830
And the training signal here is you want the decoded output

1352
01:01:37,830 --> 01:01:42,710
to match the encoded input as close as possible.

1353
01:01:42,710 --> 01:01:46,190
Now this is also used.

1354
01:01:46,190 --> 01:01:48,270
So this is used as one way to get

1355
01:01:48,270 --> 01:01:51,310
a low-dimensional representation of an image.

1356
01:01:51,310 --> 01:01:54,110
This type of encoder-decoder architecture, this

1357
01:01:54,110 --> 01:01:57,530
is built into things like variational autoencoders.

1358
01:01:57,530 --> 01:02:00,590
Masked autoencoders are increasingly widely used,

1359
01:02:00,590 --> 01:02:04,990
though those use attention as opposed

1360
01:02:04,990 --> 01:02:08,270
to convolution as a way to share information over space.

1361
01:02:12,003 --> 01:02:13,670
You can take these different components,

1362
01:02:13,670 --> 01:02:14,795
and you can break them out.

1363
01:02:14,795 --> 01:02:18,830
So we talked about differential programming.

1364
01:02:18,830 --> 01:02:20,730
If you train an encoder-decoder architecture,

1365
01:02:20,730 --> 01:02:23,470
you could use the encoder to build some representation

1366
01:02:23,470 --> 01:02:24,330
of input images.

1367
01:02:24,330 --> 01:02:27,810
But you can also use the decoder to generate images.

1368
01:02:27,810 --> 01:02:29,890
So these components can be used separately

1369
01:02:29,890 --> 01:02:32,028
and can be valuable separately.

1370
01:02:32,028 --> 01:02:33,570
But another way you might want to use

1371
01:02:33,570 --> 01:02:36,370
this type of encoder-decoder structure of architecture

1372
01:02:36,370 --> 01:02:42,010
would be to do something like get out semantic segmentation

1373
01:02:42,010 --> 01:02:46,157
because you want an output space that has spatial extent,

1374
01:02:46,157 --> 01:02:48,490
doesn't necessarily need to be the exact same resolution

1375
01:02:48,490 --> 01:02:50,530
as the input, though it can be.

1376
01:02:50,530 --> 01:02:53,330
But you want something that has a spatial structure

1377
01:02:53,330 --> 01:02:57,730
in the output space as opposed to just like a single category.

1378
01:02:57,730 --> 01:03:01,410
Now this seems nice.

1379
01:03:01,410 --> 01:03:06,030
But it turns out that this is both a blessing and a curse,

1380
01:03:06,030 --> 01:03:07,370
this bottleneck in the middle.

1381
01:03:07,370 --> 01:03:09,370
It's forcing the model to learn something that's

1382
01:03:09,370 --> 01:03:12,210
kind of semantically useful, but it also

1383
01:03:12,210 --> 01:03:15,890
means that often it's not possible with an architecture

1384
01:03:15,890 --> 01:03:18,450
that just has downsampling, then upsampling

1385
01:03:18,450 --> 01:03:21,850
to get a really precise output that's

1386
01:03:21,850 --> 01:03:24,338
the same size as the input because you've lost

1387
01:03:24,338 --> 01:03:25,630
a lot of the spatial structure.

1388
01:03:25,630 --> 01:03:27,300
You've lost a lot of the information

1389
01:03:27,300 --> 01:03:30,260
about the fine-grained details in that spatial structure.

1390
01:03:30,260 --> 01:03:33,763
And so an alternative would be to take an approach

1391
01:03:33,763 --> 01:03:36,180
to doing image-to-image, like semantic segmentation, where

1392
01:03:36,180 --> 01:03:38,340
you don't lose any spatial structure.

1393
01:03:38,340 --> 01:03:41,220
So here you do convolution, ReLU, convolution, Softmax,

1394
01:03:41,220 --> 01:03:43,900
is a very, very simple version of that.

1395
01:03:43,900 --> 01:03:46,400
But every single one of these inputs,

1396
01:03:46,400 --> 01:03:48,700
because you're keeping the entire dimension,

1397
01:03:48,700 --> 01:03:51,100
this can be really computationally expensive.

1398
01:03:51,100 --> 01:03:54,420
So the size of this model would be really huge.

1399
01:03:54,420 --> 01:03:57,060
So to bridge and get the best of both worlds, where

1400
01:03:57,060 --> 01:04:00,680
you're keeping a lot of the high-resolution structure

1401
01:04:00,680 --> 01:04:03,340
but you're also building models that are computationally

1402
01:04:03,340 --> 01:04:05,060
efficient and that are forced to learn

1403
01:04:05,060 --> 01:04:07,940
something that's kind of semantically meaningful

1404
01:04:07,940 --> 01:04:09,485
is the U-net.

1405
01:04:09,485 --> 01:04:10,860
So probably many of you have seen

1406
01:04:10,860 --> 01:04:12,740
this or different variations of this.

1407
01:04:12,740 --> 01:04:15,180
But the big intuition here is we have

1408
01:04:15,180 --> 01:04:17,580
what are called these skip connections

1409
01:04:17,580 --> 01:04:22,180
across the different encoder and decoder structures

1410
01:04:22,180 --> 01:04:23,740
of this architecture.

1411
01:04:23,740 --> 01:04:25,600
So you actually have a connection

1412
01:04:25,600 --> 01:04:29,280
that takes whatever the size is, and it just

1413
01:04:29,280 --> 01:04:34,280
takes that via the identity and lets it be skipped over

1414
01:04:34,280 --> 01:04:35,560
towards the output.

1415
01:04:35,560 --> 01:04:37,240
And what this means is the model is not

1416
01:04:37,240 --> 01:04:40,680
forced to explicitly try to shove

1417
01:04:40,680 --> 01:04:43,320
a bunch of fine-grained spatial detail

1418
01:04:43,320 --> 01:04:46,360
into some very low rank, non-spatially dimensional

1419
01:04:46,360 --> 01:04:48,600
embedding, which is somewhat impossible.

1420
01:04:48,600 --> 01:04:52,120
It's able to learn the semantic details through this information

1421
01:04:52,120 --> 01:04:55,280
bottleneck, but then it also gets a lot of the spatial detail

1422
01:04:55,280 --> 01:04:57,560
through those skipped connections.

1423
01:04:57,560 --> 01:04:58,900
And this is really powerful.

1424
01:04:58,900 --> 01:05:00,460
U-net is a really old architecture,

1425
01:05:00,460 --> 01:05:02,835
and it is still one of the most widely used architectures

1426
01:05:02,835 --> 01:05:04,220
for semantic segmentation.

1427
01:05:04,220 --> 01:05:09,200
It's used all the time in things like remote sensing,

1428
01:05:09,200 --> 01:05:12,160
like land cover mapping, for example.

1429
01:05:12,160 --> 01:05:14,320
And this idea of a skipped connection, this

1430
01:05:14,320 --> 01:05:18,360
actually is a really useful computational tool.

1431
01:05:18,360 --> 01:05:20,600
And it's also one of the fundamental underpinnings

1432
01:05:20,600 --> 01:05:22,880
of what we call the ResNet architecture.

1433
01:05:22,880 --> 01:05:25,820
So ResNet was invented by Kaiming He, who is also

1434
01:05:25,820 --> 01:05:27,780
a professor now here at MIT.

1435
01:05:27,780 --> 01:05:30,980
And this architecture is amazing.

1436
01:05:30,980 --> 01:05:33,660
We still use this all the time, and it's like 10 years

1437
01:05:33,660 --> 01:05:35,500
old at this point.

1438
01:05:35,500 --> 01:05:38,740
But really, one of the important things that was recognized

1439
01:05:38,740 --> 01:05:40,320
is essentially--

1440
01:05:40,320 --> 01:05:42,580
so one cool intuition about ResNet

1441
01:05:42,580 --> 01:05:45,200
is every single layer has this skipped connection.

1442
01:05:45,200 --> 01:05:47,220
So it has this identity pathway that

1443
01:05:47,220 --> 01:05:52,500
lets you choose whether you want to run the input

1444
01:05:52,500 --> 01:05:57,060
data through that layer or just skip it, just take this identity

1445
01:05:57,060 --> 01:06:00,300
mapping and just pass it through to the next layer.

1446
01:06:00,300 --> 01:06:02,220
So one kind of cool intuition about this

1447
01:06:02,220 --> 01:06:04,580
is this actually means that this is

1448
01:06:04,580 --> 01:06:08,660
a model that can learn something about its architecture.

1449
01:06:08,660 --> 01:06:10,760
It can learn an optimal depth.

1450
01:06:10,760 --> 01:06:13,340
Because you can essentially say, I

1451
01:06:13,340 --> 01:06:15,923
want to just pass this through maybe three of these layers.

1452
01:06:15,923 --> 01:06:17,340
And then the rest of the time, I'm

1453
01:06:17,340 --> 01:06:20,860
just going to take these skip connections out to the output.

1454
01:06:20,860 --> 01:06:23,160
And so then the model itself can implicitly

1455
01:06:23,160 --> 01:06:26,640
learn the optimal depth for the task that you want to learn.

1456
01:06:26,640 --> 01:06:29,960
So one kind of cool intuition, I think, about resonance

1457
01:06:29,960 --> 01:06:32,720
is that they give the model the flexibility

1458
01:06:32,720 --> 01:06:35,560
to learn something optimal about the architecture

1459
01:06:35,560 --> 01:06:38,800
without needing to test out explicitly

1460
01:06:38,800 --> 01:06:42,080
training a bunch of different architectures side by side.

1461
01:06:42,080 --> 01:06:44,920
And these residual connections essentially

1462
01:06:44,920 --> 01:06:48,260
are just x out equals function of x in,

1463
01:06:48,260 --> 01:06:51,840
so mapping it through that layer plus the input dimension itself.

1464
01:06:51,840 --> 01:06:54,482
And if you want to actually change the dimensionality,

1465
01:06:54,482 --> 01:06:56,440
so you want to have something like downsampling

1466
01:06:56,440 --> 01:06:58,520
within a ResNet, then you can also

1467
01:06:58,520 --> 01:07:02,440
have another set of linear weights that downsamples in that

1468
01:07:02,440 --> 01:07:04,260
skip connection.

1469
01:07:04,260 --> 01:07:05,460
Cool.

1470
01:07:05,460 --> 01:07:08,460
And this is a really, really powerful tool.

1471
01:07:08,460 --> 01:07:09,240
Yeah?

1472
01:07:09,240 --> 01:07:11,160
AUDIENCE: So with these skip connections,

1473
01:07:11,160 --> 01:07:14,560
is the skip becoming another channel that's also like

1474
01:07:14,560 --> 01:07:18,160
an output channel in the middle, or is it like actually choosing

1475
01:07:18,160 --> 01:07:27,380
one or the other/

1476
01:07:27,380 --> 01:07:31,720
SARA BEERY: So it's kind of choosing one or the other.

1477
01:07:34,300 --> 01:07:37,300
So the residual connection is essentially--

1478
01:07:37,300 --> 01:07:42,723
you assume that the output of your layer

1479
01:07:42,723 --> 01:07:44,640
and the residual connection are the same size.

1480
01:07:44,640 --> 01:07:47,860
And then you're just adding them together.

1481
01:07:47,860 --> 01:07:51,180
But there's different implementations

1482
01:07:51,180 --> 01:07:54,840
of this that will have some of learned weight.

1483
01:07:54,840 --> 01:07:57,900
So, OK, maybe you learn to completely ignore that identity.

1484
01:07:57,900 --> 01:08:00,740
That same type of skipped connection

1485
01:08:00,740 --> 01:08:04,300
is also something that gets surfaced via self-attention

1486
01:08:04,300 --> 01:08:06,100
in transformers, which we'll talk

1487
01:08:06,100 --> 01:08:08,040
about more in the transformer architecture.

1488
01:08:08,040 --> 01:08:10,060
But essentially, just this ability

1489
01:08:10,060 --> 01:08:14,500
to learn how to combine information is valuable.

1490
01:08:14,500 --> 01:08:19,253
If it is just an addition or a concatenation or however

1491
01:08:19,253 --> 01:08:20,920
you want to do that skip connection, one

1492
01:08:20,920 --> 01:08:24,340
of the cool things about linear layers or convolutional layers,

1493
01:08:24,340 --> 01:08:25,960
which are constrained linear layers,

1494
01:08:25,960 --> 01:08:30,220
is essentially even if you just add them together,

1495
01:08:30,220 --> 01:08:32,960
there's some version of the next layer that could basically

1496
01:08:32,960 --> 01:08:37,560
learn how to ignore certain dimensions, that input.

1497
01:08:37,560 --> 01:08:39,288
But yeah.

1498
01:08:39,288 --> 01:08:41,080
Like, for example, here, if you do actually

1499
01:08:41,080 --> 01:08:43,477
want to change the dimensionality, that W layer,

1500
01:08:43,477 --> 01:08:45,060
that could be learned to be all zeros.

1501
01:08:45,060 --> 01:08:47,200
So you could just not keep any of that at all

1502
01:08:47,200 --> 01:08:50,499
if that's what the model wants to learn.

1503
01:08:50,499 --> 01:08:52,080
Cool.

1504
01:08:52,080 --> 01:08:55,319
So far, we've talked about two-dimensional or

1505
01:08:55,319 --> 01:08:57,160
three-dimensional grids.

1506
01:08:57,160 --> 01:08:59,120
But one of the things that, of course, comes up

1507
01:08:59,120 --> 01:09:04,319
all the time in the real world are additional dimensionalities.

1508
01:09:04,319 --> 01:09:07,000
One really simple one is time.

1509
01:09:07,000 --> 01:09:09,700
So here, if you think about convolutions in time,

1510
01:09:09,700 --> 01:09:11,920
so maybe one version of this is you actually

1511
01:09:11,920 --> 01:09:14,920
have just a single scalar valued input, maybe something

1512
01:09:14,920 --> 01:09:17,340
like temperature at a temperature sensor.

1513
01:09:17,340 --> 01:09:19,783
And then you can think about doing convolutions over time.

1514
01:09:19,783 --> 01:09:22,200
So instead of doing convolutions over a spatial dimension,

1515
01:09:22,200 --> 01:09:24,580
you could take convolutions over a temporal dimension

1516
01:09:24,580 --> 01:09:27,140
and build some representation with time

1517
01:09:27,140 --> 01:09:29,979
as one of the dimensions of that convolution.

1518
01:09:29,979 --> 01:09:32,700
And now if you actually have something like video--

1519
01:09:32,700 --> 01:09:34,420
so, for example, now you actually

1520
01:09:34,420 --> 01:09:36,993
have an image with red, green, and blue channels.

1521
01:09:36,993 --> 01:09:38,660
And then you have these different frames

1522
01:09:38,660 --> 01:09:40,899
of an image over time.

1523
01:09:40,899 --> 01:09:44,580
You can think about building like what

1524
01:09:44,580 --> 01:09:49,180
we'll call another tensor representation or this block

1525
01:09:49,180 --> 01:09:53,359
representation where you have the spatial extent.

1526
01:09:53,359 --> 01:09:57,260
And here we're now using that third channel to represent time.

1527
01:09:57,260 --> 01:09:59,640
But then you also have RGB for each of these.

1528
01:09:59,640 --> 01:10:02,300
So it's actually a four dimensional input.

1529
01:10:02,300 --> 01:10:06,300
And then you can think of building these 3D convolutions

1530
01:10:06,300 --> 01:10:10,780
that actually convolve over space and time.

1531
01:10:10,780 --> 01:10:14,060
And then your output would be maybe a lower dimensional

1532
01:10:14,060 --> 01:10:15,960
representation that would actually

1533
01:10:15,960 --> 01:10:18,320
have those space and time dimensions for each

1534
01:10:18,320 --> 01:10:20,120
of your tensors.

1535
01:10:20,120 --> 01:10:21,658
And this is a type of architecture.

1536
01:10:21,658 --> 01:10:24,200
This type of 3D convolutions is often used when you're trying

1537
01:10:24,200 --> 01:10:27,880
to do something like action recognition in video.

1538
01:10:27,880 --> 01:10:30,200
Now, one thing that this gives you is it

1539
01:10:30,200 --> 01:10:33,280
makes you shift invariant in time.

1540
01:10:33,280 --> 01:10:35,280
But you can imagine actually that for some types

1541
01:10:35,280 --> 01:10:36,780
of recognition tasks, you don't want

1542
01:10:36,780 --> 01:10:38,420
to be shift invariant in time.

1543
01:10:38,420 --> 01:10:40,960
You don't want like picking a cup up

1544
01:10:40,960 --> 01:10:43,600
to be the same action as putting a cup down.

1545
01:10:43,600 --> 01:10:45,953
And I mean, they're--

1546
01:10:45,953 --> 01:10:48,620
ignoring the motion between, if you had a really low frame rate,

1547
01:10:48,620 --> 01:10:50,880
if you had this frame and that frame,

1548
01:10:50,880 --> 01:10:52,980
and then maybe you had that frame and this frame,

1549
01:10:52,980 --> 01:10:55,320
you want the fact that one happened before the other

1550
01:10:55,320 --> 01:10:56,180
to matter.

1551
01:10:56,180 --> 01:10:59,840
That translation invariance is not something you always want.

1552
01:10:59,840 --> 01:11:02,300
And so if you don't want to be shift invariant,

1553
01:11:02,300 --> 01:11:05,180
there's a couple of different approaches that we can use.

1554
01:11:05,180 --> 01:11:07,940
So one, use an architecture that's not shift invariant,

1555
01:11:07,940 --> 01:11:09,400
so something like an MLP.

1556
01:11:09,400 --> 01:11:12,400
There the position in time would actually matter.

1557
01:11:12,400 --> 01:11:14,740
But like we said, these are it's quite difficult

1558
01:11:14,740 --> 01:11:17,180
to get these MLPs to learn really complex functions

1559
01:11:17,180 --> 01:11:18,940
of that almost infinite data.

1560
01:11:18,940 --> 01:11:23,220
So another option, and one that is really shown to be a winner,

1561
01:11:23,220 --> 01:11:26,980
is this idea of adding location information

1562
01:11:26,980 --> 01:11:29,300
to the input to the convolutional filters

1563
01:11:29,300 --> 01:11:32,420
through something that we call a positional encoding.

1564
01:11:32,420 --> 01:11:36,940
So here what we do is you have your input signal.

1565
01:11:36,940 --> 01:11:40,600
And then you also have this separate positional encoding,

1566
01:11:40,600 --> 01:11:42,280
which is something you construct.

1567
01:11:42,280 --> 01:11:43,822
This is not something that's learned.

1568
01:11:43,822 --> 01:11:46,220
It's actually constructed and added to the input.

1569
01:11:46,220 --> 01:11:48,180
And you learn an additional weight

1570
01:11:48,180 --> 01:11:50,180
that incorporates that positional encoding

1571
01:11:50,180 --> 01:11:52,260
as part of your convolution.

1572
01:11:52,260 --> 01:11:54,500
And so there you're doing the same thing

1573
01:11:54,500 --> 01:11:56,600
that you might with a normal convolutional layer,

1574
01:11:56,600 --> 01:11:58,475
but you're adding this additional information

1575
01:11:58,475 --> 01:11:59,517
from position.

1576
01:11:59,517 --> 01:12:01,100
And so here we might want to introduce

1577
01:12:01,100 --> 01:12:04,860
the concept of what gets sometimes called a neural field.

1578
01:12:04,860 --> 01:12:09,340
So a field from physics, it can be a varying physical quantity

1579
01:12:09,340 --> 01:12:12,540
of both spatial and temporal coordinates.

1580
01:12:12,540 --> 01:12:15,110
And a neural field is a field that

1581
01:12:15,110 --> 01:12:19,773
is parameterized maybe fully or in part by a neural network.

1582
01:12:19,773 --> 01:12:21,190
So there's different ways that you

1583
01:12:21,190 --> 01:12:23,270
can think about doing this and representing

1584
01:12:23,270 --> 01:12:24,950
these neural fields.

1585
01:12:24,950 --> 01:12:27,910
But one way to think about it-- so SIREN

1586
01:12:27,910 --> 01:12:30,630
is an example of one of these neural fields, that model we

1587
01:12:30,630 --> 01:12:31,990
talked about earlier.

1588
01:12:31,990 --> 01:12:33,630
This is a convolutional neural network

1589
01:12:33,630 --> 01:12:37,930
that's applied per pixel to map from a coordinate grid,

1590
01:12:37,930 --> 01:12:41,270
so now the input is the positional encoding in an image,

1591
01:12:41,270 --> 01:12:42,750
to a color.

1592
01:12:42,750 --> 01:12:46,550
So you're actually learning to model a single image

1593
01:12:46,550 --> 01:12:49,870
by learning a functional mapping from a position in the image

1594
01:12:49,870 --> 01:12:51,092
to a color.

1595
01:12:51,092 --> 01:12:52,550
And the way that they're doing that

1596
01:12:52,550 --> 01:12:54,810
is through these sinusoidal things.

1597
01:12:54,810 --> 01:12:56,590
And so here what you actually do is

1598
01:12:56,590 --> 01:12:59,010
you take-- the input is actually just the position,

1599
01:12:59,010 --> 01:13:03,210
so in some coordinate space, and these could be explicit.

1600
01:13:03,210 --> 01:13:04,970
They could be batched up.

1601
01:13:04,970 --> 01:13:07,830
Or they also could be continuous because the idea

1602
01:13:07,830 --> 01:13:12,010
is that this is something that's mapping from some of position

1603
01:13:12,010 --> 01:13:12,750
to an output.

1604
01:13:12,750 --> 01:13:17,610
So now you run these inputs through a model

1605
01:13:17,610 --> 01:13:19,370
that's just taking x and y.

1606
01:13:19,370 --> 01:13:24,010
And then you're outputting the color of that value.

1607
01:13:24,010 --> 01:13:26,050
And this can take continuous coordinates

1608
01:13:26,050 --> 01:13:29,890
as input, which means that we can then do something

1609
01:13:29,890 --> 01:13:33,730
like think about learning these functional

1610
01:13:33,730 --> 01:13:38,090
representations of input space as opposed to only gridwise

1611
01:13:38,090 --> 01:13:39,610
representations.

1612
01:13:39,610 --> 01:13:43,250
And this idea is the fundamental underpinning

1613
01:13:43,250 --> 01:13:46,650
of quite a popular architecture recently called

1614
01:13:46,650 --> 01:13:49,970
NeRF, which is essentially a convolutional network applied

1615
01:13:49,970 --> 01:13:53,010
to a map from a five-dimensional coordinate grid, which

1616
01:13:53,010 --> 01:13:56,170
is a position and direction of the camera

1617
01:13:56,170 --> 01:13:57,730
relative to the scene.

1618
01:13:57,730 --> 01:14:02,510
And then it gives you an output of color and volumetric density.

1619
01:14:02,510 --> 01:14:06,330
And this allows us to then actually generate

1620
01:14:06,330 --> 01:14:10,790
what that same object would look like from different positions

1621
01:14:10,790 --> 01:14:13,590
than what was in the original training data.

1622
01:14:13,590 --> 01:14:15,510
So what that actually looks like is

1623
01:14:15,510 --> 01:14:17,990
you can take some images of a scene

1624
01:14:17,990 --> 01:14:21,750
and then generate these essentially walkthroughs

1625
01:14:21,750 --> 01:14:22,492
of scenes.

1626
01:14:22,492 --> 01:14:24,950
And these have actually gotten better and better over time.

1627
01:14:24,950 --> 01:14:26,750
And one of the ways that this is really--

1628
01:14:26,750 --> 01:14:28,250
one of the fundamental underpinnings

1629
01:14:28,250 --> 01:14:31,150
of this idea of building these appropriate positional

1630
01:14:31,150 --> 01:14:32,670
encodings.

1631
01:14:32,670 --> 01:14:34,350
Cool.

1632
01:14:34,350 --> 01:14:36,430
NeRFs are really fun to play with.

1633
01:14:36,430 --> 01:14:38,590
Recently, some people at Woods Hole

1634
01:14:38,590 --> 01:14:41,430
made a NeRF for under the sea that handles the way

1635
01:14:41,430 --> 01:14:42,770
that light bends under water.

1636
01:14:42,770 --> 01:14:44,390
Very neat.

1637
01:14:44,390 --> 01:14:47,990
So concluding remarks here.

1638
01:14:47,990 --> 01:14:50,950
Convolution is really just a fundamental operation

1639
01:14:50,950 --> 01:14:54,070
for image processing or actually for processing anything

1640
01:14:54,070 --> 01:14:56,250
that has some structure.

1641
01:14:56,250 --> 01:14:58,590
So this might be something like time

1642
01:14:58,590 --> 01:15:04,010
or even time, space, and multiple input dimensions.

1643
01:15:04,010 --> 01:15:05,950
So say you wanted to use convolution

1644
01:15:05,950 --> 01:15:10,050
over hyperspectral satellite imagery taken once

1645
01:15:10,050 --> 01:15:13,050
a week from the entire Earth.

1646
01:15:13,050 --> 01:15:15,465
Essentially, what it means is you're just chopping up

1647
01:15:15,465 --> 01:15:17,090
the image into patches, and then you're

1648
01:15:17,090 --> 01:15:19,230
applying the same function to each patch,

1649
01:15:19,230 --> 01:15:22,410
but you're doing it through this slide and sum operation.

1650
01:15:22,410 --> 01:15:24,410
And this concept appears in basically

1651
01:15:24,410 --> 01:15:26,270
almost all modern architectures.

1652
01:15:26,270 --> 01:15:29,440
It appears in CNNs, but also things like transformers,

1653
01:15:29,440 --> 01:15:31,690
though then the patch-wise operation isn't necessarily

1654
01:15:31,690 --> 01:15:34,190
convolution, use attention instead,

1655
01:15:34,190 --> 01:15:36,290
things like NeRFs and more.

1656
01:15:36,290 --> 01:15:38,810
So, today, we talked about building better architectures

1657
01:15:38,810 --> 01:15:39,930
and why.

1658
01:15:39,930 --> 01:15:42,910
We talked about convolutional layers, pyramids,

1659
01:15:42,910 --> 01:15:44,810
and receptive fields.

1660
01:15:44,810 --> 01:15:46,690
We went through at a very high level

1661
01:15:46,690 --> 01:15:48,450
some of these different ideas that

1662
01:15:48,450 --> 01:15:50,663
built out different types of architectures.

1663
01:15:50,663 --> 01:15:53,330
And then we talked about neural fields and positional encodings,

1664
01:15:53,330 --> 01:15:55,622
though we'll talk a lot more about positional encodings

1665
01:15:55,622 --> 01:15:57,890
in the transformers lecture.

1666
01:15:57,890 --> 01:15:58,732
Any final questions?

1667
01:15:58,732 --> 01:16:00,690
And if you are going to leave during questions,

1668
01:16:00,690 --> 01:16:04,170
just be quiet so that we can address them.

1669
01:16:04,170 --> 01:16:05,050
Yeah?

1670
01:16:05,050 --> 01:16:07,693
AUDIENCE: Could you use NeRFs or upscaling?

1671
01:16:07,693 --> 01:16:09,610
SARA BEERY: Could you use NeRFs for upscaling?

1672
01:16:09,610 --> 01:16:10,110
Yes.

1673
01:16:10,110 --> 01:16:11,212
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

1674
01:16:11,212 --> 01:16:12,170
SARA BEERY: Yeah, yeah.

1675
01:16:12,170 --> 01:16:15,230
So one of the ways that people have

1676
01:16:15,230 --> 01:16:18,305
used NeRFs is to increase the resolution of, let's

1677
01:16:18,305 --> 01:16:19,930
say, like a point cloud representation,

1678
01:16:19,930 --> 01:16:22,078
not exactly like the original NeRFs architecture,

1679
01:16:22,078 --> 01:16:23,870
but there's been adaptations of it that try

1680
01:16:23,870 --> 01:16:26,950
to increase spatial resolution.

1681
01:16:26,950 --> 01:16:29,430
Yeah.

1682
01:16:29,430 --> 01:16:32,190
Any other questions?

1683
01:16:32,190 --> 01:16:33,270
Yes?

1684
01:16:33,270 --> 01:16:34,850
And then quiet, please, you guys.

1685
01:16:34,850 --> 01:16:35,810
Thank you so much.

1686
01:16:35,810 --> 01:16:38,730
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

1687
01:16:40,666 --> 01:16:41,470
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1688
01:16:41,470 --> 01:16:45,470
So something like strided convolutions,

1689
01:16:45,470 --> 01:16:48,470
that's actually explicitly doing some form of pooling

1690
01:16:48,470 --> 01:16:51,550
and downsampling together.

1691
01:16:51,550 --> 01:16:54,070
So one way you could think about it is like pooling

1692
01:16:54,070 --> 01:16:57,410
is essentially the same as running a convolutional layer.

1693
01:16:57,410 --> 01:16:59,050
But now you're not learning weights.

1694
01:16:59,050 --> 01:17:03,950
You're just taking, for example, like the maximum function

1695
01:17:03,950 --> 01:17:05,250
or that average function.

1696
01:17:05,250 --> 01:17:06,833
That's like that's the function that's

1697
01:17:06,833 --> 01:17:08,410
applied on that local patch.

1698
01:17:08,410 --> 01:17:10,630
So essentially, it's a constrained version,

1699
01:17:10,630 --> 01:17:13,010
a nonlearned version of a convolutional layer.

1700
01:17:13,010 --> 01:17:14,050
Yeah.

1701
01:17:14,050 --> 01:17:18,030
AUDIENCE: And then downsampling is like [INAUDIBLE]?

1702
01:17:18,030 --> 01:17:20,350
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1703
01:17:20,350 --> 01:17:21,730
Yes?

1704
01:17:21,730 --> 01:17:25,050
AUDIENCE: Could you explain a little bit on the neural fields?

1705
01:17:25,050 --> 01:17:25,970
[INAUDIBLE]?

1706
01:17:29,330 --> 01:17:30,770
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1707
01:17:30,770 --> 01:17:32,970
So the way that you train it is you

1708
01:17:32,970 --> 01:17:35,490
take a bunch of different images of a scene

1709
01:17:35,490 --> 01:17:36,670
from different positions.

1710
01:17:36,670 --> 01:17:38,650
So often, the training data for NeRFs

1711
01:17:38,650 --> 01:17:41,770
will be someone taking a video, like walking around

1712
01:17:41,770 --> 01:17:43,530
an object, for example.

1713
01:17:43,530 --> 01:17:46,250
And now you have a bunch of examples where now you're

1714
01:17:46,250 --> 01:17:49,690
going to estimate the relative position to the scene.

1715
01:17:49,690 --> 01:17:54,410
And then now you can estimate the position and the direction

1716
01:17:54,410 --> 01:17:56,750
from the input image.

1717
01:17:56,750 --> 01:17:59,330
Now for any given point, that's your training data.

1718
01:17:59,330 --> 01:18:04,550
And the output is the color of the image at that pixel.

1719
01:18:04,550 --> 01:18:10,350
AUDIENCE: Is that generalizable, or is just used for picture

1720
01:18:10,350 --> 01:18:12,670
to video only used for that?

1721
01:18:12,670 --> 01:18:15,150
SARA BEERY: It can be used for a lot of different things.

1722
01:18:15,150 --> 01:18:18,970
So I will say that only just recently, like last week,

1723
01:18:18,970 --> 01:18:21,510
I saw the first kind of large-scale paper that was

1724
01:18:21,510 --> 01:18:26,510
taking this concept of NeRF, which gives you this creation

1725
01:18:26,510 --> 01:18:28,283
of video that you can interpolate between,

1726
01:18:28,283 --> 01:18:30,950
and then actually trying to move that directly to something that

1727
01:18:30,950 --> 01:18:32,990
was 3D modeling the scene.

1728
01:18:32,990 --> 01:18:36,690
Because the output of NeRF is not a 3D model of the scene.

1729
01:18:36,690 --> 01:18:42,030
It's what the image would look like taken from a different

1730
01:18:42,030 --> 01:18:45,250
direction, which is not the same as a 3D model of the scene.

1731
01:18:45,250 --> 01:18:49,310
And, actually, a lot of real-world applications we would

1732
01:18:49,310 --> 01:18:53,310
like to actually be able to use sparse monocular image

1733
01:18:53,310 --> 01:18:56,120
representations to get out a robust 3D model of the scene.

1734
01:18:56,120 --> 01:18:57,870
And so they've just started to bring those

1735
01:18:57,870 --> 01:19:02,110
two things together in recent work from Ben Mildenhall,

1736
01:19:02,110 --> 01:19:04,450
I think, was the person.

1737
01:19:04,450 --> 01:19:07,910
I will say these are also really not super robust.

1738
01:19:07,910 --> 01:19:10,170
You need a lot of input data.

1739
01:19:10,170 --> 01:19:12,170
So classical NeRF, like if you only

1740
01:19:12,170 --> 01:19:15,210
have a few sparse images of a scene,

1741
01:19:15,210 --> 01:19:18,090
it can be really hard to fit a NeRF appropriately.

1742
01:19:18,090 --> 01:19:21,250
And the other thing is it works really well

1743
01:19:21,250 --> 01:19:23,710
with stuff that is not moving.

1744
01:19:23,710 --> 01:19:28,050
But if you try and do it with something like a waving tree

1745
01:19:28,050 --> 01:19:32,850
because the actual thing itself won't-- the math

1746
01:19:32,850 --> 01:19:36,530
doesn't necessarily optimize because the thing that

1747
01:19:36,530 --> 01:19:39,730
you're taking images of is changing position over time.

1748
01:19:39,730 --> 01:19:41,870
That makes it really hard to fit these models.

1749
01:19:41,870 --> 01:19:42,370
Yeah.

1750
01:19:42,370 --> 01:19:42,850
AUDIENCE: Thank you so much.

1751
01:19:42,850 --> 01:19:43,810
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1752
01:19:43,810 --> 01:19:44,312
Yes?

1753
01:19:44,312 --> 01:19:45,770
AUDIENCE: What about problems where

1754
01:19:45,770 --> 01:19:48,890
the input is not just a picture, but a picture and constants

1755
01:19:48,890 --> 01:19:51,210
like scalar numbers?

1756
01:19:51,210 --> 01:19:53,130
[INAUDIBLE] contribute to how the picture

1757
01:19:53,130 --> 01:19:54,770
should be understood.

1758
01:19:54,770 --> 01:19:57,850
SARA BEERY: So maybe like additional metadata.

1759
01:19:57,850 --> 01:20:01,340
So for example, I might have a picture and a GPS location

1760
01:20:01,340 --> 01:20:02,883
where the picture was taken.

1761
01:20:02,883 --> 01:20:04,300
So there's a lot of different ways

1762
01:20:04,300 --> 01:20:06,420
that we try to incorporate that side information

1763
01:20:06,420 --> 01:20:08,848
into these architectures.

1764
01:20:08,848 --> 01:20:10,640
And you can see lots of different examples.

1765
01:20:10,640 --> 01:20:12,900
But some really obvious stuff is you can just

1766
01:20:12,900 --> 01:20:15,520
run your image through that first convolutional layer,

1767
01:20:15,520 --> 01:20:17,860
and then you can have a separate type

1768
01:20:17,860 --> 01:20:21,820
of convolution or representation of that positional encoding.

1769
01:20:21,820 --> 01:20:24,640
And you can either concatenate it to the entire input image.

1770
01:20:24,640 --> 01:20:27,140
You can introduce it slightly later in the network.

1771
01:20:27,140 --> 01:20:28,598
There's a lot of different ways you

1772
01:20:28,598 --> 01:20:31,580
can append additional information into the network

1773
01:20:31,580 --> 01:20:32,860
during training.

1774
01:20:32,860 --> 01:20:33,740
Yeah.

1775
01:20:33,740 --> 01:20:34,980
Yeah.

1776
01:20:34,980 --> 01:20:36,380
Cool.

1777
01:20:36,380 --> 01:20:37,120
Thanks, guys.

1778
01:20:37,120 --> 01:20:38,260
Oh yeah?

1779
01:20:38,260 --> 01:20:43,420
AUDIENCE: When you say there's [INAUDIBLE] video,

1780
01:20:43,420 --> 01:20:47,420
can you just add additional channels for each frame versus

1781
01:20:47,420 --> 01:20:49,860
just doing a 3D convolution [INAUDIBLE]?

1782
01:20:52,577 --> 01:20:54,160
SARA BEERY: Is there a big difference.

1783
01:20:54,160 --> 01:20:57,860
So if you add if you add channels for all

1784
01:20:57,860 --> 01:21:01,040
of the different frames in the video, essentially,

1785
01:21:01,040 --> 01:21:03,060
that is 3D convolution.

1786
01:21:03,060 --> 01:21:05,560
Yeah, because you're taking those channels

1787
01:21:05,560 --> 01:21:09,120
and you're maybe running your patch-wise operation almost

1788
01:21:09,120 --> 01:21:10,475
like a sliding window over time.

1789
01:21:10,475 --> 01:21:13,100
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I guess like you don't add another [INAUDIBLE].

1790
01:21:17,760 --> 01:21:19,700
SARA BEERY: So sorry, I'm a bit confused.

1791
01:21:19,700 --> 01:21:22,600
So if you have an image model that's

1792
01:21:22,600 --> 01:21:26,920
taking an RGB and height and width,

1793
01:21:26,920 --> 01:21:29,000
now you take that image model and now you're

1794
01:21:29,000 --> 01:21:35,080
just saying like stack it over the different frames.

1795
01:21:35,080 --> 01:21:38,200
How would you not add another dimension

1796
01:21:38,200 --> 01:21:39,520
if you have a time dimension?

1797
01:21:39,520 --> 01:21:41,800
AUDIENCE: Like RGB channels, so you just

1798
01:21:41,800 --> 01:21:44,300
add more RGB [INAUDIBLE] for each frame.

1799
01:21:44,300 --> 01:21:47,040
SARA BEERY: Oh, I see.

1800
01:21:47,040 --> 01:21:48,140
You could do that.

1801
01:21:48,140 --> 01:21:51,280
It would get really expensive.

1802
01:21:51,280 --> 01:21:54,600
And I think one of the things that we definitely

1803
01:21:54,600 --> 01:21:58,180
see with things like video--

1804
01:21:58,180 --> 01:22:01,980
very rarely are those convolutions

1805
01:22:01,980 --> 01:22:04,287
in the temporal space worth it computationally.

1806
01:22:04,287 --> 01:22:06,620
So if you actually look at a lot of the state of the art

1807
01:22:06,620 --> 01:22:10,020
models for video, often, they are downsampling

1808
01:22:10,020 --> 01:22:13,060
in the temporal space because you can actually

1809
01:22:13,060 --> 01:22:16,700
recognize, like most actions through what

1810
01:22:16,700 --> 01:22:18,580
we call maybe a bag of images.

1811
01:22:18,580 --> 01:22:20,160
So you don't need every single image.

1812
01:22:20,160 --> 01:22:22,160
And the difference between the individual frames

1813
01:22:22,160 --> 01:22:23,540
is really small.

1814
01:22:23,540 --> 01:22:26,900
And so like Alyosha Efros has a nice paper

1815
01:22:26,900 --> 01:22:29,780
where they talk about the complexity of actions

